consiSt("nm",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of New Mexico","d":"Court of last resort. Reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary certiorari (Rule 12-502), and takes direct appeals in cases with a sentence of death or life imprisonment, Public Regulation Commission appeals, appeals from grants of habeas corpus, and other matters reserved to it (Rule 12-102(A)). It also makes the court rules (the NMRA) and controls bar admission, pro hac vice registration, and lawyer discipline."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"New Mexico Court of Appeals","d":"Hears all other appeals on the record (Rule 12-102(B)). Sorts each case onto a general, summary, or legal calendar after reading the docketing statement (Rule 12-210). Also reviews district-court decisions in administrative appeals by discretionary certiorari (Rule 12-505)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts (13 judicial districts)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts. The judiciary describes 34 district courts in 13 judicial districts. Civil practice follows the Rules of Civil Procedure for the District Courts (Rules 1-001 et seq.), plus each district’s local rules (LR1 to LR13). District courts also hear de novo appeals from magistrate and metropolitan courts (Rule 1-072) and statutory administrative appeals (Rule 1-074)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrate Courts, Bernalillo County Metropolitan Court, Probate Courts, Municipal Courts","d":"The judiciary lists 43 magistrate courts, the Bernalillo County Metropolitan Court (Albuquerque), 33 county probate courts, and 78 municipal courts. Each has its own rule set (Rule Sets 1B, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8). Civil judgments from magistrate and metropolitan courts are appealed to district court within 15 days (Rule 1-072)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Narrow anti-SLAPP statute: only speech at public hearings or meetings in quasi-judicial proceedings","d":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1 (Laws 2001, ch. 218) covers only actions for money damages based on conduct or speech “in connection with a public hearing or public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding” before a tribunal or decision-making body of a political subdivision. A “public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding” includes meetings or presentations before state, city, town, or village councils, planning commissions, and review boards or commissions (§ 38-2-9.1(D)). This is not a general public-participation statute like California’s. Section 38-2-9.2 states the legislative findings.","c":"NMSA 1978, §§ 38-2-9.1(A), (D), 38-2-9.2","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: a special motion, Rule 12 motion, or summary judgment, heard on a priority basis","d":"A covered action is subject to a special motion to dismiss, a motion for judgment on the pleadings, or a motion for summary judgment, which the court must consider “on a priority or expedited basis.” The statute sets no discovery stay, no burden-shifting test, and no deadline for a hearing. The annotations report that courts apply it only where the speech or conduct was in connection with a qualifying proceeding. Examples: a district-court hearing on a recall petition is judicial, not quasi-judicial (Cordova v. Cline, 2013-NMCA-083). Campaign mailers are not covered (Chandler v. Advance N.M. Now PAC, 2021-NMCA-017). Statements to a federal agency are not covered (Ferebee v. Hume, 2021-NMCA-012).","c":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(A); annotations to § 38-2-9.1 (2026 NMSA)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory if the motion is filed within 90 days of the answer and granted","d":"If the defendant raises the statute’s protection as an affirmative defense, and the court grants a motion to dismiss, for judgment on the pleadings, or for summary judgment that was filed within ninety days after the moving party’s answer, the court shall award reasonable attorney fees and costs. If the court finds the special motion or summary-judgment motion frivolous or solely intended to cause delay, it shall award fees and costs to the party prevailing on the motion. The annotations report that fees are a sanction, cover appellate work, and carry no prejudgment interest (Cordova v. Cline, 2021-NMCA-022).","c":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(B); annotations (Cordova v. Cline, 2021-NMCA-022)","v":true},{"t":"Expedited appeal for any party","d":"Any party has a right to an expedited appeal from an order on the special motions, or from the trial court’s failure to rule on an expedited basis. The annotations report that the appeal covers rulings on whether the statute applies (Ferebee v. Hume, 2021-NMCA-012) and is limited to speech-based defenses (Valenzuela v. My Way Holdings, LLC, 2024-NMCA-009). The statute sets no separate appeal deadline, so the general rules apply.","c":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(C); annotations","v":true},{"t":"Not available in federal court; no UPEPA found","d":"Per the annotation, the Tenth Circuit held § 38-2-9.1 procedural and inapplicable in federal diversity cases (Los Lobos Renewable Power, LLC v. Americulture, Inc., 885 F.3d 659 (10th Cir. 2018)). The current NMSA Chapter 38 (compiled November 2026) contains no Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, and the 2026 table of newly enacted sections lists none. 2025 session laws were not separately checked, but a 2025 enactment would normally appear in the current Chapter 38.","c":"Annotation to NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.2; NMSA Ch. 38 (2026); Laws 2026 new-sections table","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is a State Bar registration, not a court motion","d":"A lawyer licensed in another U.S. jurisdiction, or before the highest court of record of any country, may perform legal services only in association with an active New Mexico State Bar member in good standing. File a registration certificate with the State Bar for each matter, naming the proceeding and the local counsel. With it, certify good standing (attaching a certificate of good standing from every jurisdiction of admission), no discipline, no revoked pro hac vice admission, and submission to New Mexico rules and discipline. Registration information is public. Prior discipline must be reported to the Disciplinary Board.","c":"Rule 24-106(A)–(C) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Fees: $500 first registration, $375 each later one; 5-per-year cap","d":"The first registration certificate in a calendar year costs $500 (non-refundable). Each later one that year costs $375. No lawyer may register more than five times in any calendar year. The fee is waived for government lawyers acting for a government, legal-aid lawyers acting for indigent clients, and lawyers representing an indigent client for no fee.","c":"Rule 24-106(A), (D) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"In district court: file an affidavit, and local counsel must attend everything","d":"After complying with Rule 24-106, the out-of-state lawyer files, with the first paper filed (or as soon as practicable after being retained), an affidavit of good standing and Rule 24-106 compliance. The lawyer is then deemed admitted, with no separate motion or order. New Mexico counsel must sign the first pleading or motion and appear on every later paper, and is deemed to sign all later pleadings for Rule 1-011 purposes. New Mexico counsel must also be present in person at all proceedings unless the court excuses that. The court may end the out-of-state lawyer’s appearance for noncompliance.","c":"Rule 1-089.1 NMRA","v":true},{"t":"In the appellate courts: same association requirement","d":"Counsel admitted elsewhere may, after complying with Rule 24-106, sign briefs and papers and argue orally only in association with New Mexico counsel. New Mexico counsel signs the first paper filed in the appellate court, and that counsel’s name and address must appear on every later paper.","c":"Rule 12-302(E) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and ADR without registration","d":"Temporary litigation-related work must comply with Rule 24-106 and be done with an actively participating New Mexico lawyer. Some work needs no Rule 24-106 registration: arbitration, mediation, or other ADR reasonably related to the lawyer’s home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice), work arising from the home-state practice, in-house work, and federally authorized services. In transactions involving issues specific to New Mexico law, the lawyer must associate New Mexico counsel.","c":"Rule 16-505(E), (F) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Malpractice-insurance notice applies to pro hac vice lawyers too","d":"A lawyer without professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate must give the client a prescribed written notice and obtain a signed acknowledgment, and must keep the record for six years. “Lawyer” expressly includes lawyers provisionally admitted under Rule 24-106.","c":"Rule 16-104(C) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are allowed but must be labeled","d":"Limited representation is allowed with informed consent (Rule 16-102(C)). In district court, file a “Limited Entry of Appearance” describing the limit, and put the limit and a service address for the client in every signature block. When the limited work is complete, a notice of withdrawal is enough; otherwise withdrawal needs a court order. The lawyer must disclose the scope to the tribunal (Rule 16-303(E)). In the appellate courts, state the limit on the cover page and in the signature block (Rule 12-302(B)).","c":"Rules 1-089, 12-302(B), 16-102(C), 16-303(E) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: mandatory for lawyers in the appellate courts; set by local rule in district courts","d":"In the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals, lawyers must file through the approved EFS, register, and keep a working email address. Self-represented parties may not e-file. The EFS charges no fee, but docket fees must be paid by check within 5 days after the case is accepted, or the case may be dismissed (Rule 12-307.2). In district court, e-filing is mandatory where a Supreme Court-approved local rule requires it. Each e-filing carries an $8 electronic services fee ($12 to file and serve), and a filing submitted before midnight counts as filed that day (Rule 1-005.2). Which districts mandate e-filing depends on their local rules, which were not collected.","c":"Rules 1-005.2, 12-307.2 NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Full admission: UBE transfer, reciprocity, and limited licenses","d":"Methods include UBE score transfer (a passing score within five years plus the ethics exam), a reciprocal license (active practice in a reciprocal state for 5 of the past 7 years, among other conditions), and limited licenses for in-house counsel, public employees and public defenders, legal-services lawyers, military spouses, law professors, and former federal employees. A “NextGen Bar Exam” amendment to these rules (approved October 30, 2026, effective December 31, 2026) was not collected.","c":"Rules 15-302, 15-303, 15-304 to 15-310 NMRA","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"New Mexico Rules of Professional Conduct, Rules 16-100 to 16-805 NMRA","d":"Based on the ABA Model Rules but renumbered with a 16- prefix: Model Rule 1.6 is Rule 16-106, MR 1.18 is Rule 16-118, MR 8.4 is Rule 16-804. In effect since January 1, 1987, and amended since.","c":"Rule Set 16 NMRA (compiler’s note)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory malpractice-insurance disclosure","d":"This is not in the Model Rules. A lawyer without coverage of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate (deductibles included) must give the client the exact written notice in the rule, get a signed acknowledgment, renew the notice if coverage lapses during the matter, and keep the records for six years. Full-time judges, single-entity in-house counsel, and government lawyers are exempt.","c":"Rule 16-104(C) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory succession plan","d":"This is not in the Model Rules. Every active New Mexico licensee needs a written succession plan naming an “assisting lawyer” who consents in writing. The plan must say where client files, passwords, and trust-account records are kept. Clients must be told the plan exists, and compliance is certified each year on the State Bar registration statement.","c":"Rule 16-119 NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Harassment and discrimination is misconduct; one-party recording addressed","d":"Rule 16-804(G) (similar to MR 8.4(g)) makes knowing or negligent harassment or discrimination in conduct related to the practice of law misconduct. An amendment effective December 31, 2026 adds protected classes, including color, ancestry, pregnancy, and military status. Its new commentary says a lawyer who creates or takes part in a one-party recording does not violate Rule 16-804(C) unless the conduct involves dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.","c":"Rule 16-804(G) NMRA; Order No. S-1-RCR-2026-00196 (eff. Dec. 31, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Trust records kept five years; limited-appearance disclosure to the tribunal","d":"Trust-account records must be kept for five years after the representation ends (Rule 16-115(A)). A lawyer appearing in a limited manner must disclose the scope of representation to the tribunal (Rule 16-303(E)), a New Mexico addition to MR 3.3.","c":"Rules 16-115(A), 16-303(E) NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Pro bono: 50-hour aspiration, or 1% of income from December 31, 2026","d":"Lawyers should aspire to 50 hours of pro bono service a year and must certify their hours or contribution each year. Through December 30, 2026, the financial alternative is $500 a year (or the sliding table in the rule). From December 31, 2026, the alternative is 1% of the lawyer’s annual income from practicing law in New Mexico.","c":"Rule 24-108 NMRA (as amended by Order No. S-1-RCR-2025-00152, eff. Dec. 31, 2026); Rule 16-601 NMRA","v":true}],"style":"State of New Mexico","home":"https://www.nmcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Excuse the judge in 10 days, and before asking the judge for anything","d":"The peremptory-excusal clock is 10 days: from notice of assignment for plaintiffs, and from the first Rule 1-012 filing for defendants. The 120-day outer cap and the bar after any hearing or request to the judge apply on top of that. Co-defendants sharing a firm get only one excusal between them.","c":"Rule 1-088.1 NMRA"},{"t":"Counting days: 10 or fewer skips weekends; 11 or more counts every day","d":"Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays. Periods of 11 days or more count every day. Three days are added for mail service, but not to appeal, rehearing, or certiorari deadlines.","c":"Rules 1-006, 12-308, 12-201(A)(2), 12-404(A), 12-502(B) NMRA"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days from filing in the clerk’s office, with no mail days","d":"The 30 days run from when the judgment is filed in the district court clerk’s office. Only a timely motion that could affect finality restarts the clock (Rule 1-060(B) motions only if filed within 30 days of judgment). An order granting a new trial is not appealable and makes the earlier judgment non-appealable. Extensions must be sought within 30 days after the deadline, can add at most 30 days, and a notice of appeal must still be filed within those 30 days even if the motion is pending.","c":"Rule 12-201(A), (D), (E) NMRA"},{"t":"Trial counsel owes the docketing statement in 30 days","d":"In the Court of Appeals, trial counsel must file the docketing statement within 30 days after the notice of appeal, with the docket fee. Conclusory issue statements are rejected. Issues and preservation must be stated specifically, because on the summary calendar the court decides on the docketing statement and record alone.","c":"Rules 12-208(A), (B), (D), (H), 12-210(D) NMRA"},{"t":"Answer a proposed summary disposition within 20 days","d":"If the case is placed on the summary calendar, the parties have 20 days from service of the notice of proposed summary disposition to file a memorandum in opposition or support. The limit is 35 pages or 11,000 words, and no new issues may be raised outside the docketing statement unless the court allows an amendment. There is no oral argument.","c":"Rule 12-210(D) NMRA"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP fees depend on filing within 90 days of the answer","d":"Mandatory fees follow only if the protection is pleaded as an affirmative defense and the dispositive motion is filed within ninety days after the movant’s answer. The statute reaches only speech at public hearings or meetings in quasi-judicial proceedings before state or local bodies, and it does not apply in federal diversity cases.","c":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(A), (B), (D)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: 5 registrations a year, and local counsel must physically attend","d":"Register with the State Bar (not the court) for each matter: $500 for the first registration and $375 for each later one in a calendar year, with a maximum of five a year. Then file the Rule 1-089.1 affidavit with your first paper. New Mexico counsel must be present in person at all proceedings unless excused.","c":"Rules 24-106(A), (D), 1-089.1 NMRA"},{"t":"Uninsured lawyers, including pro hac vice lawyers, must give written notice","d":"If your malpractice coverage is below $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate, you must give the client the prescribed notice and get a signed acknowledgment. This applies to lawyers practicing under Rule 24-106.","c":"Rule 16-104(C)(1), (3) NMRA"},{"t":"Summary-judgment papers: numbered facts, 15-day response","d":"The movant lists numbered material facts with record citations. The opponent responds within 15 days with numbered disputed facts keyed to the movant’s numbers, and any fact not specifically controverted is deemed admitted. The reply is due within 15 days. Opposed motions generally must recite that concurrence was sought.","c":"Rules 1-056, 1-007.1 NMRA"},{"t":"Shorter post-trial and responsive-pleading clocks than federal practice","d":"Motions for new trial or to alter or amend judgment are due within 30 days after entry of judgment. After a Rule 1-012 motion is denied, the answer is due in 10 days. A jury demand is due within 10 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, with a $150 or $300 jury fee, or the jury is waived.","c":"Rules 1-059, 1-012(A), 1-038 NMRA"},{"t":"Two years of inactivity can mean dismissal with prejudice","d":"Any party may move to dismiss a claim with prejudice if no significant action toward trial or final disposition has been taken within 2 years after filing. Without a scheduling order, the court may also dismiss without prejudice after 180 days of inactivity, with 30 days to seek reinstatement.","c":"Rule 1-041(E) NMRA"},{"t":"Appellate amendments take effect December 31, 2026","d":"Orders approved October 30, 2026 amend Rules 12-202, 12-208, 12-209, 12-211, 12-503, 12-505, 12-601 to 12-603 and add Rules 12-208.1, 12-211.1, and 12-502.1, for cases filed on or after December 31, 2026. Examples: in criminal, delinquency, and Children’s Code cases the notice of appeal is no longer served on the appellate court (other cases still serve it); the district court clerk must file the record proper within 5 days (was 14) after receiving the docketing statement; and the appellant pays record costs within 5 days after the clerk files the statement of costs. Check which version governs your case.","c":"Order No. S-1-RCR-2023-00005; Rules 12-202(E), 12-209(C) NMRA as amended (eff. Dec. 31, 2026)"}],"judge":[{"t":"One peremptory excusal per party; 10-day windows, 120-day hard stop","d":"Each party may excuse one district judge without cause. A plaintiff must file the excusal within 10 days after service of notice of assignment of the first judge. A defendant must file within 10 days after filing its first Rule 1-012 pleading or motion. After a reassignment, any party has 10 days after the clerk’s notice. No excusal is allowed more than 120 days after the judge was assigned. The right is lost once the party attends a hearing before that judge or asks the judge to act (other than on free process or indigency). Aligned co-parties with the same lawyer or firm, joint pleadings, close family ties, or corporate-affiliate or agency relationships count as one party, unless the judge finds their interests sufficiently diverse. Serve notice on all parties. The excused judge rules on timeliness and validity.","c":"Rule 1-088.1 NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Statutory peremptory challenge","d":"By statute, each party to a civil or criminal action may peremptorily challenge one district judge, and the judge “shall proceed no further.” In workers’ compensation cases, the employer and its insurer count as one party. A separate statute sets a 10-day affidavit deadline (after the cause is at issue, after the jury-demand time expires, or after the judge is assigned, whichever is later). The rule sets its own deadlines, so follow Rule 1-088.1 and do not rely on the statute alone. How the two fit together was not checked beyond the annotations.","c":"NMSA 1978, §§ 38-3-9, 38-3-10","v":true},{"t":"Recusal for cause; judges who cannot be peremptorily excused","d":"Recusal for cause is always available, and a judge whose impartiality may reasonably be questioned must file a recusal (Rule 1-088.1). A judge designated by the chief justice, and that judge’s successor, may be excused only under N.M. Const. art. VI, § 18, not peremptorily (Rule 1-088). Water judges and stream-adjudication judges are also excluded (Rules 1-071.5, 1-077.2). The constitutional text was not collected.","c":"Rules 1-088, 1-088.1, 1-071.5, 1-077.2 NMRA","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"No broader speech protection found in collected texts","d":"Beyond § 38-2-9.1, the collected rules and Chapter 38 contain no general speech-protection procedure (no UPEPA). Defamation-specific provisions in Chapter 38 (such as § 38-2-9 on truth and mitigation) were not analyzed.","c":"NMSA 1978, Ch. 38 (2026)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Docketing statement and calendar system in the Court of Appeals","d":"Within 30 days after the notice of appeal, trial counsel (not necessarily appellate counsel) files a docketing statement that sets out the issues, how each was preserved, and the authorities. The court then assigns the case to a summary, general, or legal calendar. On the summary calendar there are no briefs: you get 20 days to answer a notice of proposed summary disposition.","c":"Rules 12-208, 12-210 NMRA","v":true},{"t":"Either side can make a Rule 1-068 offer, with double costs","d":"Unlike FRCP 68, a claimant can serve an offer of settlement, but not until 120 days after the defending party’s responsive pleading. Offers must be served more than 10 days before trial and accepted within 10 days. If the claimant’s rejected offer is beaten, the defending party pays double the post-offer costs (no attorney fees).","c":"Rule 1-068 NMRA","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Rules of Appellate Procedure, Rule Set 12 NMRA (2026 compilation on NMOneSource.com, amendments through December 31, 2025), plus the Supreme Court’s amendments approved October 30, 2026 and effective December 31, 2026 (Order No. S-1-RCR-2023-00005 and related orders: Rules 12-202, 12-208, 12-208.1, 12-209, 12-211, 12-211.1, 12-502.1, 12-503, 12-505). Also Rules 1-054, 1-059, 1-062, 1-072 and 1-074 NMRA (Rule Set 1, 2026 compilation) and NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1. Official texts from nmonesource.com and supremecourt.nmcourts.gov; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in New Mexico: 30 days, a docketing statement, then a calendar","d":"Most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals. The Supreme Court takes direct appeals only in narrow categories (death or life sentences, Public Regulation Commission appeals, grants of habeas corpus, and matters reserved to it) and otherwise reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary certiorari. You file the notice of appeal in the district court within 30 days after the judgment is filed in the clerk’s office. Within 30 more days, trial counsel files a docketing statement in the Court of Appeals. The court then puts the case on a summary calendar (no briefs; decided on the docketing statement and record), a general calendar (full transcript and briefs), or a legal calendar (briefs, no transcript). Amendments effective December 31, 2026 change record and service details for cases filed on or after that date."},"groups":[["Where the appeal goes",[{"t":"Court of Appeals unless the case is in a Supreme Court category","d":"Appeals go to the Supreme Court from district courts that imposed a sentence of death or life imprisonment, from the Public Regulation Commission, from grants of habeas corpus, and in matters reserved to the Supreme Court by the Constitution or by Supreme Court order or rule. All other appeals on the record go to the Court of Appeals.","c":"Rule 12-102 NMRA"},{"t":"Magistrate and metropolitan court civil judgments go to district court first","d":"Appeal by filing a notice of appeal in the district court of the county within 15 days after the lower-court judgment is filed. Other parties have 10 days after service of the first notice, or the original period if later. The 3-day mailing period does not apply. A jury demand must be filed with the notice, and no later than 30 days after service of the notice.","c":"Rule 1-072 NMRA"},{"t":"Agency decisions: district court first, then certiorari to the Court of Appeals","d":"Statutory administrative appeals go to the district court within 30 days after the agency’s final decision, unless a statute or ordinance sets another time (Rule 1-074). Review of the district court’s decision is by petition for certiorari to the Court of Appeals, filed within 30 days after the district court’s final action, with no 3 mail days (Rule 12-505).","c":"Rules 1-074, 12-505 NMRA"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal within 30 days after the judgment is filed","d":"File the notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 30 days after the judgment or order is filed in the district court clerk’s office. The deadline is 10 days for the state’s appeals from suppression orders under § 39-3-3(B)(2). The 3-day mail extension does not apply. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before the judgment is filed counts as filed on the day the judgment is filed.","c":"Rules 12-201(A), 12-202(A) NMRA"},{"t":"Contents, attachment, and service","d":"The notice must name each appellant and appellee, give appellate counsel’s name and address if different, and name the court to which the appeal is taken. Attach a copy of the judgment or order showing its date. In civil cases, serve the appellate court, the trial judge, the court monitor or reporter, and trial counsel for every other party. Serve unrepresented parties by mail at their last known address. Disclose any related appeals in the docketing statement.","c":"Rule 12-202(B), (C), (E)(3), (F), (G) NMRA (as amended eff. Dec. 31, 2026)"},{"t":"Mailed or couriered notices","d":"A notice sent by mail or commercial courier is timely on the day it is received if its certificate of service states that it was postmarked or courier-stamped at least one day before the due date.","c":"Rule 12-201(F) NMRA"},{"t":"Extensions: good cause before the deadline, excusable neglect within 30 days after","d":"Move in the district court before the deadline (good cause) or within 30 days after it (excusable neglect or circumstances beyond the appellant’s control). A motion filed later cannot be granted. An extension may not run more than 30 days past the original due date. The notice of appeal itself must be filed within those 30 days even if the motion is still pending.","c":"Rule 12-201(E) NMRA"}]],["Tolling and cross-appeals",[{"t":"Motions that could affect finality restart the full 30 days","d":"A timely motion that could affect finality restarts the full appeal period, which then runs from the filing of an order expressly disposing of the last such motion. Qualifying motions include motions under Rules 1-050(B), 1-052(D), and 1-059, and § 39-1-1. A Rule 1-060(B) motion qualifies only if filed within 30 days after the judgment. A motion to reconsider qualifies if filed within the time to appeal. For motions that are automatically denied by rule or statute, the time runs from the earlier of an express order or the automatic denial. Withdrawing the motion starts the clock on the date the notice of withdrawal is filed.","c":"Rule 12-201(D)(1)–(3) NMRA"},{"t":"Premature notices ripen; new-trial orders are not appealable","d":"A notice of appeal filed while a tolling motion is pending becomes effective when the motion is decided, denied, or withdrawn. It then brings up the ruling on the motion without a separate notice. An order granting a new trial in a civil case is not appealable and makes the earlier judgment non-appealable.","c":"Rule 12-201(D)(4), (5) NMRA"},{"t":"Post-trial motion deadlines in district court","d":"Motions for new trial or to alter, amend, or reconsider a final judgment must be filed within 30 days after entry of judgment.","c":"Rule 1-059 NMRA"},{"t":"Cross-appeal: 14 days after the first notice is served","d":"Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was served, or within the original time, whichever ends later. The first to file is the appellant. An appellee may raise issues to support affirmance, or issues to be decided only if the court reverses, without cross-appealing.","c":"Rule 12-201(B), (C) NMRA"}]],["Docketing statement and calendar assignment",[{"t":"Docketing statement within 30 days, filed by trial counsel, with the docket fee","d":"Unless the court orders otherwise, trial counsel prepares and files the docketing statement in the Court of Appeals within 30 days after the notice of appeal (or after a premature notice becomes effective). It must cover the nature of the case, timeliness, the material facts, each issue and how it was preserved, the authorities for and against with the standard of review, whether the proceedings were audio recorded, and related appeals. Conclusory issue statements are not accepted. The docket fee accompanies it, and no response is allowed. A cross-appellant files its own docketing statement within 30 days after its notice. Rule 1-054(B) partial-judgment certification and interlocutory applications follow separate paths.","c":"Rule 12-208 NMRA"},{"t":"Record proper","d":"When the district court clerk receives the docketing statement, the clerk numbers and transmits the record proper (the docketed pleadings, motions, and papers). Under the amendment for cases filed on or after December 31, 2026, the record is due within 5 days (formerly 14), and the appellant pays the costs within 5 days after the clerk files the statement of costs.","c":"Rule 12-209 NMRA (as amended eff. Dec. 31, 2026)"},{"t":"Summary calendar: 20 days to oppose a proposed disposition","d":"No briefs or transcripts are filed. The court issues a notice of proposed summary disposition, and parties have 20 days from service to file a memorandum in opposition or support. The limit is 35 pages, or 11,000 words or 1,200 monospaced lines with a compliance statement. Arguments are limited to issues in the docketing statement, but a motion to amend can be combined with the memorandum. No oral argument.","c":"Rule 12-210(D) NMRA"},{"t":"General calendar: transcript, then 45/45/20-day briefing","d":"For audio-recorded proceedings, the district clerk sends the recording within 15 days after the general calendar assignment, and the appellant proves payment arrangements within 5 days. If proceedings were not recorded, the appellant designates the transcript or files a notice of non-designation within 15 days. The brief in chief is due 45 days after notice that the transcripts are filed (or after the notice of non-designation). The answer brief is due 45 days after service of the brief in chief, and the reply 20 days after the answer.","c":"Rules 12-210(C), 12-211 NMRA"},{"t":"Legal calendar: 30/30/20-day briefing, no transcript","d":"For cases decided on legal issues only, the brief in chief is due 30 days after service of the calendar notice, the answer brief 30 days after the brief in chief, and the reply 20 days after the answer. No oral argument unless ordered.","c":"Rule 12-210(E) NMRA"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Length: 35 pages or 11,000 words; reply 15 pages or 4,400 words","d":"Briefs in chief, answer briefs, and amicus briefs: 35 pages, or 11,000 words (1,200 monospaced lines) with a statement of compliance giving the word count and the software used. Reply briefs: 15 pages or 4,400 words (480 lines). No attachments to briefs. New issues not in the docketing statement may be raised unless the appellee would be prejudiced.","c":"Rule 12-318(A)(1), (F), (G) NMRA"},{"t":"Cross-appeal briefing","d":"The appellee files its answer brief and its brief in chief on cross-appeal together, as separate documents: 45 days after the brief in chief on the general calendar, or 20 days on the legal calendar. The appellant’s reply and cross-answer follow on the same 45- or 20-day schedule. The cross-appellant may reply within 20 days.","c":"Rule 12-318(I) NMRA"}]],["Interlocutory and expedited appeals",[{"t":"Certified interlocutory orders: application within 15 days","d":"If the district court’s order contains the statutory certification (§ 39-3-4(A) in civil cases), file an application for interlocutory appeal with the appellate court clerk within 15 days after entry, with the docket fee and no docketing statement. The application must state the controlling question of law, the substantial ground for difference of opinion, and how an immediate appeal will advance the case. Responses are due in 15 days, and a reply needs leave sought within 7 days. Granting the application stays the district court unless ordered otherwise. No 3 mail days.","c":"Rule 12-203 NMRA"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP orders","d":"Any party may take an expedited appeal from an order on an anti-SLAPP special motion, or from the court’s failure to rule on one expeditiously.","c":"NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(C)"},{"t":"Stays and supersedeas","d":"Execution may issue when judgment is entered unless the court orders otherwise. A supersedeas bond approved by the district court stays a money judgment, and appeals by the state and its subdivisions stay automatically (Rule 1-062). Apply to the district court first. The appellate court reviews that ruling on motion for abuse of discretion and may allow up to 15 more days to post a bond (Rule 12-207).","c":"Rules 1-062, 12-207 NMRA"}]],["Rehearing and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Rehearing within 15 days","d":"A motion for rehearing may be filed within 15 days after the disposition is filed, with no 3 mail days. It must state the overlooked or misapprehended points, without argument. A brief is required if the court relied on a point no party raised. No response unless requested. In the Court of Appeals, any two participating judges can grant rehearing; in the Supreme Court, any three justices.","c":"Rule 12-404 NMRA"},{"t":"Certiorari to the Supreme Court within 30 days","d":"File within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ final action (its decision, or the ruling on a timely rehearing motion), with the docket fee and no mail days. Grounds: conflict with Supreme Court or Court of Appeals precedent, a significant constitutional question, or an issue of substantial public interest. Limit: 10 pages, or 3,150 words or 342 monospaced lines. A conditional cross-petition is due within 15 days after service, and a response within 15 days after service or after the grant. A reply needs leave sought within 7 days. If the writ is granted from a general-calendar case, the brief in chief is due 45 days after the grant order.","c":"Rule 12-502 NMRA"},{"t":"Expedited certiorari categories (from December 31, 2026)","d":"For certiorari in abuse and neglect, termination of parental rights, delinquency, and interlocutory-application cases, the clerk sets oral argument within 60 days. Simultaneous supplemental briefs are due in 20 days (20 pages or 6,300 words), and replies are due 5 days before argument (10 pages or 3,150 words).","c":"Rule 12-502.1 NMRA (new, eff. Dec. 31, 2026)"},{"t":"Mandate","d":"The Court of Appeals’ mandate does not issue until the time for certiorari has run or certiorari is resolved, and it may be recalled within 10 days for good cause. The Supreme Court’s mandate issues 15 days after its disposition or modification. A stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court review is limited to 60 days unless extended.","c":"Rule 12-402 NMRA"}]],["E-filing",[{"t":"Mandatory EFS for lawyers; docket fees by check within 5 days","d":"Represented parties, including lawyers representing themselves, must file through the EFS. Self-represented parties may not e-file. The EFS charges no fee, but docket fees for e-filed new cases must be paid by check within 5 days after acceptance. An unpaid fee can lead to dismissal without prejudice, and reinstatement may be sought within 15 days. Electronic service is complete on transmission. If a recipient reports within 2 days that a document cannot be read, it must be served another way.","c":"Rule 12-307.2 NMRA"},{"t":"Counting time","d":"Periods of 11 days or more count every day. Periods of 10 days or less skip weekends and holidays. A deadline falling on a weekend or holiday rolls to the next business day. For e-filing, the last day ends at midnight.","c":"Rule 12-308(A) NMRA"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"30 days from filing of the judgment or order in the district court clerk’s office.\nNo 3-day mail extension. Filed in district court.","c":"Rule 12-201(A)(1)(b) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the judgment or order in the district court clerk’s office)","note":"No 3-day mail extension. Filed in district court."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","d":"14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, if later).","c":"Rule 12-201(B)(1) NMRA","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to extend time to appeal","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nExcusable neglect after the deadline. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline.","c":"Rule 12-201(E) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal deadline)","note":"Excusable neglect after the deadline. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial / to alter or amend judgment","d":"30 days from entry of judgment.\nTolls the time to appeal (Rule 12-201(D)).","c":"Rule 1-059 NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Tolls the time to appeal (Rule 12-201(D))."}},{"t":"Docketing statement (Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or the date a premature notice becomes effective).\nFiled by trial counsel, with the docket fee.","c":"Rule 12-208(B) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed by trial counsel, with the docket fee."}},{"t":"Memorandum opposing a proposed summary disposition","d":"20 days from service of the notice of proposed summary disposition.\n35 pages or 11,000 words.","c":"Rule 12-210(D)(2) NMRA","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of proposed summary disposition)","note":"35 pages or 11,000 words."}},{"t":"Brief in chief (general calendar)","d":"45 days from service of notice that transcripts are filed, or the notice of non-designation.","c":"Rule 12-210(C)(2)(a) NMRA","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of notice that transcripts are filed, or the notice of non-designation)","note":""}},{"t":"Answer brief (general calendar)","d":"45 days from service of the brief in chief.","c":"Rule 12-210(C)(2)(b) NMRA","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of the brief in chief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"20 days from service of the answer brief.\nOptional.","c":"Rule 12-210(C)(2)(c), (E)(2)(c) NMRA","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answer brief)","note":"Optional."}},{"t":"Brief in chief (legal calendar)","d":"30 days from service of the calendar assignment notice.\nAnswer brief due 30 days after the brief in chief.","c":"Rule 12-210(E)(2)(a) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the calendar assignment notice)","note":"Answer brief due 30 days after the brief in chief."}},{"t":"Application for interlocutory appeal","d":"15 days from entry of the certified interlocutory order.\nFiled in the appellate court. No mail days.","c":"Rule 12-203(A) NMRA","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the certified interlocutory order)","note":"Filed in the appellate court. No mail days."}},{"t":"Motion for rehearing","d":"15 days from filing of the appellate disposition.\nNo mail days.","c":"Rule 12-404(A) NMRA","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellate disposition)","note":"No mail days."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of certiorari to the Supreme Court","d":"30 days from final action by the Court of Appeals (decision, or ruling on a timely rehearing motion).\n10 pages or 3,150 words. No mail days.","c":"Rule 12-502(B) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (final action by the Court of Appeals)","note":"10 pages or 3,150 words. No mail days."}},{"t":"Response to certiorari petition / conditional cross-petition","d":"15 days from service of the petition (response: or the grant).","c":"Rule 12-502(G), (H) NMRA","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Certiorari to Court of Appeals in administrative cases","d":"30 days from district court’s final action.","c":"Rule 12-505(C) NMRA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (district court’s final action)","note":""}},{"t":"Appeal from magistrate or metropolitan court to district court","d":"15 days from filing of the lower-court judgment.\nTrial de novo cases.","c":"Rule 1-072 NMRA","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the lower-court judgment)","note":"Trial de novo cases."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.nmcourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Rules of Civil Procedure for the District Courts","rulesSrc":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1-001","t":"Scope of rules; definitions","s":"These rules govern procedure in all civil suits in New Mexico district courts, at law or in equity, except where the Rules of Evidence are inconsistent; unless they say otherwise, they yield to contrary statutes for special statutory or summary proceedings, are subject to Rule 23-114 NMRA (free process), and are to be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action. “Defendant” includes a respondent, “plaintiff” includes a petitioner, “process” (the means of obtaining jurisdiction to compel a person’s appearance) includes a summons and complaint or petition, a writ or warrant, and a mandate, and “service of process” means delivery as provided in Rule 1-004. The rules are titled the Rules of Civil Procedure for the District Courts and cited as “Rule 1-___ NMRA.”","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-002","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action,” as under FRCP 2.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-003","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint, and the clerk endorses on it the time, day, month and year of filing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-003.1","t":"Commencement of action; domestic relations information sheet","s":"A domestic relations information sheet in the Supreme Court-approved form must be submitted with a petition initiating a domestic relations case, a motion to reopen a closed one, and a party’s first responsive pleading, and a blank copy must be served on the respondent with the summons and petition; information drawn from it into the court system is confidential except as disclosed to the parties (unless the court orders otherwise), to state and federal agencies required by law to collect it, and to court personnel for enforcement, data collection and record keeping. The sheet has no legal effect in the action, and the clerk will still file a pleading without a complete sheet but gives written notice of the deficiency; if it is not cured within 30 days, the court may dismiss the party’s claim without prejudice, and the clerk serves the dismissal order on all parties.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-003.2","t":"Commencement of action; guardianship and conservatorship information sheet","s":"A petitioner seeking appointment of a guardian or conservator under NMSA 1978, Chapter 45, Article 5, Parts 3 or 4 must submit, on filing the petition, an information sheet substantially in the Supreme Court-approved form identifying persons entitled to notice and access to court records. Under an amendment effective December 31, 2026, such an action must be initiated by a petition for court appointment that names only one alleged incapacitated person.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-003.3","t":"Commencement of residential foreclosure action; certification of pre-filing notice required; definition","s":"Every complaint initiating a residential foreclosure action must be submitted with a certification of pre-filing notice substantially in the form of Form 4-227 NMRA, and notwithstanding Rule 1-005(F) the clerk must refuse to file a residential foreclosure complaint without it; the rule does not apply to lien foreclosures. “Residential” means property designed principally for occupancy by one to four families and occupied by the defendant as the defendant’s principal residence.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-004","t":"Process","s":"On filing of the complaint the clerk issues a summons (in the approved form, directing the defendant to serve a responsive pleading or motion within 30 days after service and naming plaintiff’s counsel or the self-represented plaintiff), which any defendant may waive, and service must be made with “reasonable diligence” rather than within a fixed period as under FRCP 4(m), by any nonparty over 18 (writs of attachment, replevin or habeas corpus by a court-designated nonparty over 18 or the sheriff), in a manner reasonably calculated to give notice; service by mail or commercial courier is allowed and complete when the defendant or authorized agent signs the receipt (including electronically). Individuals are served in a fixed priority: personally (refusal counts as service) or by signed-receipt mail or courier, then, if that fails, by delivery to a resident over 15 at the usual abode plus first-class mail, then by delivery to the person in charge at the defendant’s workplace plus first-class mail to the last known address and workplace; on motion without notice and a sworn showing that those methods failed or are impractical and that the defendant owns and, within 30 days of the motion, has used the account, address or number, the court may authorize service by social media, e-mail or text (with prescribed notice language), sent once a week for three consecutive weeks and complete on the last transmission, plus first-class mailing of the papers within 10 days of the first electronic service. The rule also specifies service on corporations, LLCs, partnerships and unincorporated associations (officer, managing or general agent, or general partner, or the person in charge at the principal office), on the state and its agencies, officers, counties, municipalities, school districts and land-grant boards, on minors, incompetent persons and fiduciaries, outside New Mexico, and in foreign countries (Hague Convention or other internationally agreed means, foreign law, letter rogatory, or the Paragraph F, G or J methods); on motion and affidavit the court may order any reasonably calculated method, and publication is allowed only by such order, in a newspaper of general circulation in the county (and where the defendant is most likely to see it), once a week for three consecutive weeks unless ordered otherwise, complete on the last publication, with prescribed notice contents including real property descriptions and unknown-heir language. The party obtaining service must promptly file proof (sheriff’s certificate, affidavit, certificate with the signed receipt for mail or courier, affidavit with copies of the messages for electronic service, or publisher’s affidavit), but failure to file proof does not affect validity, and the rule does not govern service in Criminal Records Expungement Act proceedings, which follow Rule 1-077.1(E).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-004.1","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; process","s":"In proceedings to appoint a guardian or conservator under NMSA 1978, Chapter 45, Article 5, Parts 3 and 4 (but not temporary appointments under Sections 45-5-310 or 45-5-408), the court must within 5 days of the petition set a hearing no sooner than 60 days after filing and issue a notice of hearing and rights (Form 4-999 NMRA) in lieu of a summons. The notice and petition must be served personally on the alleged incapacitated person within 11 days of issuance, by the guardian ad litem or a person over 18 who is not a party or interested person (refusal counts as service, and no other method is effective), with proof by affidavit or unsworn affirmation under Rule 1-011, and the petition may not be granted without such personal service. Interested persons entitled to notice must be served under Rule 1-005 within 11 days of service on the alleged incapacitated person, and in a conservatorship of a minor each person with legal authority over the minor must be served personally (or a court-designated person if there is none).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-005","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every written order, pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, non-ex parte motion, notice, appearance, demand, offer of settlement and similar paper must be served on every party, except that parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with pleadings asserting new or additional claims, and then in the manner of Rule 1-004; service on a represented party is made on the attorney by delivery (including fax or electronic transmission where Rules 1-005.1 or 1-005.2 permit, office or abode delivery, or a court-designated attorney location) or by first-class mail, complete upon mailing. Papers served after the complaint are filed with a certificate of service within a reasonable time, but 13 listed categories (such as discovery requests and responses, depositions, subpoenas, unopposed-motion briefs, settlement offers and Rule 1-123 disclosures) are not filed unless ordered or used, and for most of these a certificate of service must still be filed; the clerk may not refuse a paper solely for improper form, and a party filing by fax or electronically may not later submit a duplicate paper copy. The court serves its own orders and notices of hearing, and papers it serves are deemed served by mail for Rule 1-006(C) unless its certificate unambiguously says otherwise; an inmate’s filing or mailing is timely if deposited in the institution’s legal mail system within the time allowed (provable by a sworn statement creating a rebuttable presumption), and periods triggered by an inmate’s filing or service run from the date the other party receives it.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-005.1","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers by facsimile","s":"Pleadings and papers may be filed by fax to a number designated by the clerk (or the judge), with the same effect as any other filing, but direct fax filing is allowed only if no fee is required, only one copy is required, the paper is not more than 10 pages excluding the cover page unless the court approves, and it is preceded by a cover sheet with specified sender, case and page information; these limits do not apply to fax copies filed in person by an intermediary agent. A fax begun before the close of the court’s business day is filed that day (otherwise the next court business day), as shown by the court machine’s cover-page time stamp, and papers may be served by fax on a party or attorney who has listed a fax number on a filing or letterhead or agreed to fax service, complete when transmission is completed. The court may issue notices, orders and writs by fax, parties may inspect and copy any sworn paper filed or served by fax, and the clerk will conform additional copies on request.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-005.2","t":"Electronic service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"A district court may by Supreme Court-approved local rule mandate electronic filing through the approved EFS for represented parties (excluding, unless the local rule provides otherwise, certain child support, domestic violence, sealed, habeas and Children’s Court matters), attorneys must register with a working e-mail address, and self-represented parties may not e-file; documents may be served electronically on registered attorneys or those who agreed to e-mail service, complete when transmitted, but if the recipient reports within 2 days that it cannot be read, it must be re-served by another method the recipient designates. Electronic filers pay an $8.00 electronic services fee per filing transmission in a case, or $12.00 to file and serve together, while serving an already-filed document is free and the fees do not apply to entities listed in Section 34-6-40(C) NMSA 1978 or to civil legal service providers; a filing day runs from 12:01 a.m. to midnight, a transmission received before midnight on the day preceding the next business day is treated as filed on that preceding business day, and the envelope submission time counts as the filing time for limitations and deadlines even if the filing is rejected or sent to an error queue. E-filed documents are deemed signed by the filing attorney (with scanned or “/s/” signatures permitted), originals must be kept until final disposition and all appeals, the clerk converts paper filings by exempt attorneys and self-represented parties with the paper filing date preserved, proposed orders go to a court-designated e-mail address with copies to all parties (except listed “issuance documents” such as summonses, subpoenas and writs, which go through the EFS), and an attorney may petition the Supreme Court, without a docket fee, for a good-cause exemption lasting one year and renewable. Substantive rights are not affected when the EFS is down through no fault of the filer.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-006","t":"Time","s":"The rule governs computing any period in these rules, local rules, court orders or statutes unless another Supreme Court rule expressly supersedes it: for periods of 11 days or more every day is counted, while for periods of 10 days or less intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded (a difference from FRCP 6, though not for statutory pre-suit notices), the trigger day is excluded and a last day falling on a weekend or holiday rolls to the next business day, hour-based periods run continuously, and closure or unavailability of the court on the last day (or in the last hour) extends the deadline to the next open day. The last day ends at midnight for electronic filing and at the court’s scheduled closing for other filing, and legal holidays are the listed judiciary holidays (including Juneteenth, Indigenous Peoples Day and Presidents’ Day, observed the day after Thanksgiving) plus any other judiciary holiday. The court may extend time for cause on a request before the deadline or, after it, on motion showing excusable neglect, but not under Rules 1-050, 1-052, 1-059, 1-060, 1-062 or 12-201 except as those rules allow; 3 days are added (counting weekends and holidays, and rolling past a weekend or holiday) after service by mail, fax, or deposit at a court attorney location, and the court must post its regular hours.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-007","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint and third-party answer, and a reply to an answer or third-party answer only if the court orders one. A request for an order must be by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and form rules for pleadings apply to motions; demurrers, pleas and exceptions for insufficiency are abolished.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-007.1","t":"Motions; how presented","s":"Motions (other than those made at trial or as the court permits) must be in writing with particular grounds; for an unopposed motion an order approved by all parties must accompany it, and an opposed motion must recite that the movant sought all parties’ concurrence or explain why not, except for motions to dismiss, for new trial, for judgment as a matter of law, for summary judgment, or for relief under Rule 1-060(B). The movant may file a supporting brief, must attach affidavits or other evidence for facts outside the record and the proposed pleading for a motion to amend, and must file a request for hearing in the approved form with any opposed motion. Responses and supporting evidence are due within 15 days after service of the motion (or the court may rule with or without a hearing), must be separate from any counter- or cross-motion, replies are due within 15 days after service of the response, and the movant or any party then files a notice of completion of briefing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-007.2","t":"Time limit for filing motion to compel arbitration","s":"A party seeking to compel arbitration of a claim must file and serve a motion to compel no later than 10 days after service of the answer or of the last pleading directed to that claim, a deadline with no FRCP counterpart.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-008","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim for relief must contain proper allegations of venue (the county named in the complaint is taken as the intended venue), a short and plain statement of the claim showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for judgment, which may be in the alternative; unlike FRCP 8, the complaint may not allege damages in any specific monetary amount unless that is a necessary allegation. Responses must admit or deny averments (lack of knowledge operates as a denial), fairly meet their substance, and may use a general denial only when the pleader in good faith controverts everything, and listed affirmative defenses (including comparative negligence, accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, laches, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations and waiver) must be pleaded affirmatively. Averments other than the amount of damage are admitted if not denied where a response is required, alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed subject to Rule 1-011, and pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-008.1","t":"Pleadings and papers; captions","s":"Every pleading or paper filed in district court must have a caption showing “State of New Mexico, County of ___, ___ Judicial District,” the names of the parties, and a title describing the cause of action or relief requested, which has no legal effect in the action.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-009","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity, authority and legal existence need not be averred except to show jurisdiction, and a challenge must be made by specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while condition of mind may be averred generally, conditions precedent may be averred generally but denied specifically, and official acts, judgments, special damages (which must be specifically stated), time and place (which are material), and statutes (which need only be referred to generally) follow the federal model. Unlike FRCP 9, when an instrument on which a claim or defense is founded is referred to in a pleading, the original or a copy must be served with the pleading if within the party’s power or control (but need not be filed). In business-course consumer debt collection actions (excluding medical debt and loans secured by real property) the pleading must comply with Rule 1-017(E) and Form 4-226 NMRA, medical debt claims must comply with Rule 1-017(E) and Form 4-226A and include the current Superintendent of Insurance Attestation of Indigency form, the instrument underlying any consumer debt claim must be served and filed unless excused for good cause, and an answer to a medical debt claim in the form of Form 4-301A NMRA satisfies Paragraph C. An amendment effective December 31, 2026 corrects the internal cross-references so that both consumer and medical debt pleadings must comply with Subparagraph (J)(3) (copy of the instrument served and filed), without other substantive change.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-010","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title of the action, file number and Rule 1-007(A) designation, with all parties named in the complaint’s title but only the first party on each side in later pleadings. Averments go in numbered paragraphs limited as far as practicable to a single set of circumstances, separate transactions and defenses go in separate counts when that aids clarity, statements may be adopted by reference, and a written instrument attached as an exhibit is part of the pleading for all purposes.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-011","t":"Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers; sanctions; unsworn affirmations under penalty of perjury","s":"Every pleading, motion and paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or the self-represented party) with address and telephone number, and the signature certifies that the signer has read it, that to the best of the signer’s knowledge, information and belief there is good ground to support it, and that it is not interposed for delay; unlike current FRCP 11, the rule does not set out a safe-harbor procedure but provides that a paper signed to defeat the rule may be stricken as sham and false, an unsigned paper is stricken unless promptly signed, and willful violations or scandalous or indecent matter may lead to disciplinary or other action. A “signature” includes an original, a copy, a computer-generated signature or any other lawful signature. Except as provided in Rule 1-120, an unnotarized written statement has the same effect as a notarized one if it includes the date, the signer’s signature, and an affirmation under penalty of perjury under New Mexico law that it is true and correct.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-012","t":"Defenses and objections; when and how presented; by pleading or motion; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"An answer is due within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint, an answer to a cross-claim within 30 days after its service, and a reply to a counterclaim within 30 days after service of the answer (or of an order requiring a reply); if a Rule 1-012 motion is denied or deferred, or a more definite statement is served, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days, unlike the 14 days under FRCP 12. The defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a Rule 1-019 party may be raised by pre-answer motion (prepared under Rule 1-007.1), and a failure-to-state-a-claim motion or motion for judgment on the pleadings that relies on matters outside the pleadings is treated as one for summary judgment under Rule 1-056. An order granting a more definite statement must be obeyed within 10 days after notice or the pleading may be stricken, a motion to strike insufficient defenses or redundant, immaterial, impertinent or scandalous matter may be made before responding or, if no response is permitted, within 30 days after service, and the court may strike on its own at any time. Personal jurisdiction, venue, process and service defenses are waived if omitted from a consolidated Rule 1-012 motion or from the responsive pleading or an amendment as of course, failure to state a claim or to join an indispensable party may be raised as late as trial, and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction requires dismissal whenever it appears.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-013","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising out of the same transaction or occurrence that does not require parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction, unless it was already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment or similar process without personal jurisdiction and the pleader asserts no counterclaim; other claims may be asserted as permissive counterclaims, and a counterclaim may exceed or differ in kind from the opposing claim. The rules do not enlarge the right to counterclaim against the state, after-acquired or matured claims may be added by supplemental pleading with permission, and an omitted counterclaim may be added by leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect or when justice requires. Cross-claims against a coparty arising from the same transaction or occurrence or concerning property at issue (including indemnity-type claims) are permitted, additional parties needed for complete relief must be brought in if jurisdiction can be obtained without defeating jurisdiction over the action, and judgment on a counterclaim or cross-claim may be entered under Rule 1-054(B) after a separate trial under Rule 1-042(B).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-014","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may at any time serve a summons and third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer (unlike the 14 days under FRCP 14) and otherwise only on motion with notice to all parties. The third-party defendant answers under Rules 1-012 and 1-013, may assert the third-party plaintiff’s defenses and its own same-transaction claims against the plaintiff, the plaintiff may assert same-transaction claims against it, any party may move to strike, sever or separately try the third-party claim, and the third-party defendant may in turn implead others. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may bring in a third party on the same terms.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-015","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served or, if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, within 20 days after serving the pleading; otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires, or the adverse party’s written consent, and a response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining to respond to the original or 10 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded and amendments to conform to the evidence may be made at any time, even after judgment, and are freely allowed over an objection absent prejudice, with a possible continuance. An amendment arising from the same conduct, transaction or occurrence relates back; unlike FRCP 15, a motion to change the party against whom a claim is asserted filed before the limitations period runs relates back to the motion’s filing date if accompanied by a proposed amended pleading naming the new party, and one filed after the period runs relates back to the original pleading only if the new party, within the Rule 1-004(C)(2) “reasonable diligence” service period, received notice avoiding prejudice and knew or should have known it would have been sued but for a mistake of identity. The court may permit supplemental pleadings on post-pleading events even if the original pleading is defective, and every amended or supplemental complaint, answer or reply must set forth all matters in one entire pleading.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-016","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The court may hold pretrial conferences to expedite disposition, manage the case, discourage waste, improve trial preparation and facilitate settlement, and, except in categories exempted by local rule, may enter after consultation a scheduling order limiting the time to join parties, amend, file and hear motions, and complete discovery, and providing for electronically stored information, privilege clawback agreements, conference dates and a trial date not later than 18 months after the order is filed. The scheduling order must be filed as soon as practicable and no more than 120 days after the complaint is filed and may be modified only for good cause; absent such an order, the case must be set for trial no later than 18 months after the complaint is filed, though the court may extend these limits for good cause. Conferences may address 12 listed subjects (including issue simplification, admissions, limits on expert witnesses, referral to a master and settlement), an attorney with authority to stipulate must attend, the final pretrial conference must be attended by a trial attorney for each party, and the resulting pretrial order controls and after a final conference may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. For failure to obey a scheduling or pretrial order, failure to appear, substantial unpreparedness or lack of good faith, the judge may impose Rule 1-037(B)(2)(b)–(d) sanctions and must award reasonable expenses, including attorney fees, unless the noncompliance was substantially justified or an award would be unjust.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-017","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, guardians, express trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names, and where an honest mistake was made the court must allow a reasonable time for ratification, joinder or substitution with retroactive effect. Unlike FRCP 17, a wrongful death action under Section 41-2-1 NMSA 1978 must be brought by a personal representative appointed by the district court under Section 41-2-3 (by petition before or with the action), individual capacity is governed by New Mexico law and corporate capacity by the law of incorporation unless a New Mexico statute provides otherwise, and an infant or incompetent person may sue through a representative, next friend or guardian ad litem, with the court required to appoint a guardian ad litem or otherwise protect one not represented. In consumer debt claims, a collection agency may sue in its own name as assignee only through a licensed attorney and must plead specific facts showing it is the real party in interest, and any non-original creditor must file a personal-knowledge affidavit establishing the full chain of title or assignment from the original creditor, with business records attached and foundation satisfying Rule 11-803(6)(a)–(c) or a Rule 11-902(11) or (12) certification.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-018","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party asserting a claim or counterclaim may join as many legal or equitable claims as it has against an opposing party, independently or in the alternative, and multiparty, cross-claim and third-party joinder is allowed when Rules 1-019, 1-020, 1-022, 1-013 and 1-014 are satisfied. Claims formerly cognizable only after another claim concluded may be joined, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive rights, so a plaintiff may seek money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-019","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be accorded without that person or if the person claims an interest whose disposition in the person’s absence may impair its protection or expose existing parties to a substantial risk of double, multiple or inconsistent obligations; the court orders joinder, and one who refuses to join as plaintiff may be made a defendant or involuntary plaintiff. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, considering prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and whether the plaintiff will have an adequate remedy if dismissed. A pleader must name known unjoined required persons and state why they are not joined, and the rule is subject to Rule 1-023 on class actions.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-020","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the rights to relief asserted arise jointly, severally or in the alternative out of the same transaction, occurrence or series and a common question of law or fact will arise, and no party need be interested in all the relief, with judgment given according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, expense or prejudice.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-021","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may drop or add parties on motion or on its own at any stage on just terms, and any claim against a party may be severed and proceeded with separately.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-022","t":"Interpleader","s":"Persons with claims against the plaintiff that expose it to double or multiple liability may be joined as defendants and required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim, supplementing Rule 1-020 joinder. On filing, the district court takes full jurisdiction of the thing in dispute and orders all claimants to interplead within the time allowed by law for answering, with the order served as on adverse parties; unlike FRCP 22, when an affidavit shows that a claimant to property in the court’s custody is a nonresident of New Mexico, the court must order service on that claimant by publication. The decree determines disposition of the property and binds all served parties.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-023","t":"Class actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality and adequate representation, plus one of the three FRCP 23(b)-style grounds (risk of inconsistent or practically dispositive individual adjudications, generally applicable conduct warranting class-wide injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority); the court decides certification by a conditional, amendable order as soon as practicable, must give (B)(3) class members the best practicable notice, including individual notice where identifiable, with the right to opt out by a specified date and to appear through counsel, and may certify issue classes or subclasses and issue case-management and notice orders. A class action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice to all members, and the Court of Appeals may in its discretion permit an appeal of an order granting or denying certification if application is made within 15 days after entry, without staying district court proceedings unless ordered. Unlike FRCP 23, the rule requires the judgment or settlement order (or a later order) to direct any residual funds, meaning unclaimed money after all approved payments or the whole fund if distribution is impossible or impractical, to nonprofits benefiting the class or similarly situated persons, related educational entities, low-income legal services nonprofits, or the entities administering the IOLTA fund (Rule 24-109) or pro hac vice fund (Rule 24-106) for civil access to justice, though settlements need not create residual funds.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-023.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"A shareholder or member suing derivatively to enforce a right the corporation or unincorporated association has failed to enforce must file a verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or devolution by operation of law) and describing with particularity the efforts made to obtain action from the directors and, if necessary, the shareholders or members, or the reasons for not making them. The action may not proceed if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated shareholders or members, and it may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice as the court directs.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-024","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely application, a person must be allowed to intervene when a statute gives an unconditional right or when the person claims an interest in the subject property or transaction that the action may practically impair and that existing parties do not adequately represent. The court may allow intervention when a statute gives a conditional right, when the applicant’s claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the action, or by a government officer or agency administering a statute, executive order or regulation relied on by a party, considering undue delay or prejudice. The intervenor must serve a motion stating the grounds under Rule 1-005, accompanied by a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-025","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, any party or the decedent’s successor or representative may move for substitution, served with a notice of hearing on parties under Rule 1-005 and on nonparties as a summons under Rule 1-004, and the action is dismissed as to the decedent unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record by a statement served the same way; where the right survives only to or against surviving parties, the action proceeds without abatement. The court may on similarly served motion continue an action by or against the representative of a party who becomes incompetent, and after a transfer of interest the action continues by or against the original party unless the court orders the transferee substituted or joined. A public officer sued in an official capacity who dies or leaves office is automatically succeeded by the successor without abatement, misnomers not affecting substantial rights are disregarded, and the officer may be described by title.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-026","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Parties may obtain discovery of any nonprivileged information relevant to the subject matter of the action, including inadmissible information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence (a broader standard than current FRCP 26(b)(1)’s proportional “claim or defense” scope), and must provide all nonprivileged responsive information then known, but the court must limit discovery that is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative, obtainable more cheaply elsewhere, previously available, or whose burden outweighs its benefit; parties may discover trial witnesses and the substance of their testimony, persons with information and documents another party may use, and insurance agreements (but not applications), while trial-preparation materials require substantial need and inability to obtain an equivalent without undue hardship, with mental impressions protected and a person entitled to its own previous statement. Instead of FRCP 26(a)’s mandatory disclosures, testifying experts are discovered by interrogatories and requests for production (identity, subject matter, substance of facts and opinions with a summary of grounds, qualifications, reports, publications within the preceding 10 years, and cases testified in within the preceding 4 years) and may be deposed, non-testifying consulting experts are discoverable only under Rule 1-035 or exceptional circumstances, and the requesting party pays the expert a reasonable fee unless manifest injustice would result. Withheld privileged or work-product material must be expressly claimed and described, inadvertently produced material must on notice be returned, sequestered or destroyed and may be presented by motion for in camera review, protective orders are available for good cause on a motion that sets forth or attaches the disputed request (with Rule 1-037 expense shifting), discovery may proceed in any sequence and a party may not withhold responses because discovery is continuing or later deadlines exist, and responses must be seasonably supplemented if materially incomplete or incorrect. The court may call, and on any party’s motion must hold unless good cause exists not to, a discovery conference followed by an order setting the issues, plan, schedule, limits and expense allocation (including expert deadlines on request or for good cause); an amendment effective December 31, 2026 restyles the trial-preparation provisions and, to the same extent as the FRCP, extends work-product protection to drafts of expert reports or disclosures and to attorney-expert communications except those about compensation or identifying facts, data or assumptions the attorney provided that the expert considered or relied on.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-027","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party to an action that cannot yet be brought may file a verified petition in the district court of the county where any expected adverse party resides, describing the expected action, the facts to be established and reasons to perpetuate them, the expected adverse parties, and the witnesses and their expected testimony, and must serve notice and the petition at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of a Rule 1-004 summons (or as the court orders, with an appointed attorney for those not so served and Rule 1-017(C) applying to minors or incompetents). If perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice, the court orders the depositions (oral or written) and may issue Rule 1-034 and 1-035 orders, and the deposition may be used under Rule 1-032 in any later action on the same subject matter. During an appeal, or before one while the time to appeal has not expired, the district court that rendered judgment may on motion allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for further proceedings, and the rule does not limit an independent action to perpetuate testimony.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-028","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths where the examination is held or a person appointed by the court, and in a foreign country on notice before a person authorized by local or U.S. law, before a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory issued on application and notice (which need not be shown necessary, and whose evidence is not excluded merely for not being verbatim or sworn). Subject to Rule 1-029, a deposition may not be taken before a relative, employee or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of such attorney, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-029","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise or earlier orders conflict, the parties may by written stipulation provide for depositions before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner, usable like other depositions, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-030","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"After commencement any party may depose any person by oral examination, and the parties must confer in good faith on date, time and place; the plaintiff needs leave to depose before 30 days after service of the summons and complaint on any defendant (or service under Rule 1-004(F)) unless a defendant has sought discovery or the notice states with supporting facts that the deponent will be unavailable or leaving the state, though such a deposition cannot be used against a party who diligently but unsuccessfully sought counsel, and a prisoner may be deposed only by leave. At least 10 days’ written notice to every other party is required, stating the recording method (sound, sound-and-visual or stenographic at the noticing party’s cost, with other parties free to add a method at their own expense), and the notice may include a Rule 1-034 request to a party deponent, may name an organization that must designate persons to testify on described matters, and may provide for telephone or remote electronic examination; the officer makes a prescribed opening statement, examination begins at the noticed time or within 30 minutes after it, documents shown must be copied to all parties, the record may go off only by agreement, and parties may submit sealed written questions instead of attending. Objections must be concise, non-argumentative and non-suggestive, with form or foundation objections stated only as “objection — form” or “objection — foundation” unless the questioner asks for specifics, and no one may interrupt a pending question except to preserve a privilege, enforce a court limit or move to terminate; a non-expert deposition is limited to one day of 7 hours on the record unless the court or parties allow more, the court where the action is pending or where the deposition is taken may stop or limit a bad-faith or oppressive examination (with Rule 1-037(A)(4) expenses), and on request the deponent has 30 days after notice of availability to review and sign changes. The officer certifies, seals and sends the deposition to the attorney who arranged it for safekeeping, furnishes copies for reasonable charges, and produced documents may be attached as copies or originals; a party filing a deposition must promptly notify all others, the court may award expenses including attorney fees against a noticing party who fails to attend or to subpoena the witness, a deponent’s nonappearance is not willful under Rule 1-037(D) or contemptuous under Rule 1-045(E) if a protective-order motion and notice of nonappearance are filed with actual notice to all parties at least 3 days before (unless the motion is frivolous or dilatory), and the original may be destroyed once the judgment is final or the case closed.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-030.1","t":"Audiotaped and videotaped depositions","s":"Defines “stenographic recording” as simultaneous verbatim reporting and, on request, requires the noticing party to let others review and buy at reasonable cost a copy of an audio or video deposition. Unless ordered or stipulated otherwise, recorded depositions must follow standard procedures (identify attendees on the record, video only the deponent and demonstratives, neutral setting, go off the record only by agreement not unreasonably withheld, announce tape changes, keep a counter log, mark and number originals, never edit the original). If there is no stenographic transcript, the deponent gets a copy and has 30 days after receipt to sign a statement of changes in form or substance, or no changes may later be made, unless review was waived on the record. A party using such a deposition at trial must supply playback equipment and an operator.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-031","t":"Depositions on written questions","s":"After the action begins, any party may depose any person on written questions, serving the questions on every other party with a notice naming the deponent (or describing the deponent or class) and the officer; organizations may be deposed under Rule 1-030(B)(6), and a prisoner only by leave of court. Cross-questions are due within 30 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days after cross-questions, and recross-questions within 10 days after redirect, with the court able to enlarge or shorten these times for cause. The officer takes the answers and prepares, certifies, and files or mails the deposition, and the taking party must promptly notify all parties of filing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-032","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used at trial or hearings against parties who were present, represented, or had reasonable notice: for impeachment, by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent is a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee, and by any party for any purpose on stipulation or if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state (unless the offering party procured the absence), 100 miles or less away if a pre-deposition order allowed trial use and the notice said so, unable to attend due to age, illness, infirmity, or imprisonment, unable to be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances. Unlike FRCP 32, NM lets a deposition of a nearby witness (100 miles or less) be used at trial if a court order entered before the deposition permitted it and the notice stated that intent. Nonstenographic deposition testimony must be accompanied by a transcript, and in jury trials any party may require nonstenographic presentation (other than for impeachment) if available, absent good cause. Objections to notice errors must be promptly served in writing, objections to the officer’s qualification are made before or as soon as discovered, form objections to written questions must be served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after service of the last questions, and transcription or certification defects are waived unless a motion to suppress is made with reasonable promptness.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-033","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Without leave or written stipulation, a party may serve up to 50 interrogatories, including all discrete subparts, on any other party (more than FRCP 33’s 25), and additional ones are allowed consistent with Rule 1-026(B)(2); in multiparty cases the serving party must notify all appearing parties that interrogatories were served and provide copies on request. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, subject to court order or written agreement under Rule 1-029. Each interrogatory must be answered separately and fully under oath, objections must be stated with specificity or are waived absent good cause, and contention interrogatories are allowed but may be deferred. A party may answer by specifying business records, including electronically stored information, when the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for both sides.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-034","t":"Production of documents and things and entry on land for inspection and other purposes","s":"A party may request another party to produce documents, electronically stored information, or tangible things for inspection, copying, testing, or sampling, or to permit entry on land, describing items with reasonable particularity and optionally specifying the form for electronically stored information; nonparties are reached by subpoena under Rule 1-045. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or for a defendant within 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, and must state specific reasons for any objection, state the form to be used for electronically stored information, state whether the response includes all responsive materials, and describe with reasonable particularity what is withheld under each objection. Unless otherwise agreed or ordered, documents are produced as kept in the usual course of business or organized by request category, electronically stored information in its ordinary or a reasonably usable form, and only in one form.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-035","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"When the mental or physical condition, including blood group, of a party or of a person in a party’s custody or legal control is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause, with notice to the person and all parties, order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request the party causing the exam must deliver a detailed written report and reports of earlier exams of the same condition, and is then entitled to like reports from the other side; by obtaining the report or deposing the examiner, the examined party waives privilege as to others who examined the same condition. These provisions also apply to examinations by agreement unless the agreement says otherwise.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-036","t":"Requests for admissions","s":"A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within the scope of discovery, including facts, opinions, application of law to fact, and the genuineness of documents (copies attached). A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, or other time the court allows, though a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; denials must fairly meet the substance, and lack of knowledge is allowed only after reasonable inquiry. The requesting party may move to test the sufficiency of responses, with expenses under Rule 1-037(A)(4), and admissions are conclusively established for that action only unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-037","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"A party may move, on reasonable notice, to compel deposition answers, organizational designations, interrogatory answers, or inspection (evasive or incomplete answers count as failures), stating a good faith effort to resolve the dispute and setting out or attaching the requests and responses; the losing side generally pays reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees unless substantially justified, with apportionment when granted in part. Failure to obey a discovery order may lead to contempt, facts being taken as established, preclusion, striking pleadings, stays, dismissal, default judgment, and mandatory expenses unless substantially justified (contempt is not available for refusal of a physical or mental examination). A party that fails to admit a matter later proved must pay the costs of proof unless the request was objectionable, unimportant, the party reasonably believed it might prevail, or there was another good reason. A party that fails to appear for its own deposition or to respond to interrogatories or inspection requests faces the same sanctions and cannot excuse the failure as objectionable unless it sought a protective order under Rule 1-026.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-038","t":"Jury trial in civil actions","s":"A party may demand a jury by serving a written demand after the action begins and no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and filing it under Rule 1-005(E); a demand is for six jurors unless twelve are specifically demanded, and after a six-person demand any party may demand twelve within 10 days after that demand or the last pleading, whichever is later. A party demanding six jurors deposits a non-refundable $150 jury fee when filing the demand plus $150 for each later trial day, a party demanding twelve deposits $300 plus $300 per later day, and if a twelve-person demand follows a six-person one each party deposits $150 plus $150 per later day; all parties may stipulate to twelve jurors no later than 30 days before trial, splitting the fee pro rata. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges for a six-person jury and 5 for twelve (exercised jointly by co-parties unless interests differ, in which case each party gets 3 or 5), and verdicts require agreement of at least 5 of 6 or 10 of 12 jurors. Jury trial is waived by failing to demand, failing to pay fees, failing to appear, written waiver, or oral consent in open court; jury fees are taxed as costs against the losing party, are never refunded, and are reapportioned if the demanding party is dismissed or withdraws the demand.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-039","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Issues not set for jury trial under Rule 1-038 are tried by the court, but the court may on motion order a jury trial despite a party’s failure to make a timely demand. In actions not triable of right by jury, the court may use an advisory jury on motion or its own initiative, or with both parties’ consent order a jury whose verdict has the same effect as a jury of right.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-040","t":"Assignment of cases for trial and order of trial","s":"District courts set cases for trial under Rule 1-016, and a case on a trailing calendar counts as set only if its trial is likely to begin within 2 weeks after the first case on that calendar. Absent a scheduling order, any party may submit a request for trial stating the case is ready and the trial time needed, and a party who disagrees must file a response within 10 days after service explaining why and when the case will be ready; the court must mail reasonable notice of settings. Unless the court directs otherwise, trial proceeds with jury selection, opening statements (non-first parties may defer theirs), evidence in chief, response, and rebuttal (only one counsel per side examines a witness), jury instructions, and argument, with motions for directed verdict or mistrial argued outside the jury’s presence.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-041","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or other responsive pleading, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared generally; such dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits, and other voluntary dismissals require a court order and cannot override an objecting party’s pending counterclaim that cannot stand independently. A defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or to comply with rules or orders, or in a bench trial after the plaintiff rests, and such involuntary dismissals operate as adjudications on the merits except for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a Rule 1-019 party. Unlike FRCP 41, any party may move to dismiss a claim with prejudice if no significant action has been taken toward trial or final disposition within 2 years after filing (unless the claimant complies with a Rule 1-016 order or approved stipulation), and absent a scheduling order the court may dismiss without prejudice for no significant action in the previous 180 days, with reinstatement on motion within 30 days after service of the order for good cause and court review of such cases at least twice each calendar year. The court may order costs of a previously dismissed action and stay a refiled action until paid; the rule applies to all district court civil cases, including appeals from metropolitan or magistrate courts, but not to guardianship, receivership, trusteeship, conservatorship, Mental Health and Developmental Disabilities Code, Probate Code, or Children’s Code proceedings.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-042","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"When actions involving a common question of law or fact are pending within a judicial district, the court may order a joint hearing or trial, consolidate them, or make orders avoiding unnecessary cost or delay. For convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, the court may order separate trials of any claims or issues, always preserving any constitutional right to jury trial.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-043","t":"Evidence","s":"Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless another rule provides otherwise. Testimony given in any state or federal court in New Mexico may be used at a later trial or hearing of the same issue between the same parties if the witness is dead or insane, is a nonresident, or cannot be located after diligent effort (the extracted text reads “same issued,” apparently a typo for “same issue”), without limiting evidence admissible under the Rules of Evidence. Motions based on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, or wholly or partly on oral testimony or depositions.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-044","t":"Judicial notice and determination of foreign law","s":"New Mexico courts must take judicial notice of the meaning of English words and legal expressions, whatever is established by law, official acts of the federal government and the laws of the states and territories as interpreted by their highest courts, court and notary seals, the accession and official signatures of government officers, the titles, flags, and seals of recognized sovereigns, and the laws of nature, time, geography, and world political history, consulting reference works as needed and without limiting notice under the Rules of Evidence. A party intending to raise foreign-country law must give notice in the pleadings or other reasonable written notice, and the court may consider any relevant source, admissible or not, treating its determination as a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-045","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue from the court for the district where the case is pending, in the Supreme Court-approved form, signed by the clerk in blank or by a New Mexico attorney for a party, and may command testimony, production of documents, electronically stored information, or things, or inspection of premises. They may be served anywhere in the state by a nonparty at least 18 years old, with tender of one full day’s per diem and mileage under Section 10-8-4 NMSA 1978 (not prorated, paid before each additional day; not required for the state) or processing of fees for state-paid witnesses, and with prior or simultaneous notice to all parties of any pretrial production subpoena; a person may be required to attend a deposition within 100 miles of where the person resides, works, or transacts business, and a hearing or trial anywhere in the state. A person commanded to produce may not respond before 14 days after service absent court order, any party objecting must serve a written objection or motion to quash within 14 days after service, and once an objection or motion is made production may occur only by court order; the court must quash or modify a subpoena that allows unreasonable time, requires a nonparty to travel more than 100 miles (except to trial within the state), requires privileged matter, or imposes undue burden, and may protect trade secrets, unretained expert opinions, and nonparties facing substantial expense to travel over 100 miles to trial. Responding persons must produce documents as kept or organized by category, need not produce electronically stored information in more than one form or from sources not reasonably accessible absent good cause, must expressly describe privilege claims, and may claw back privileged material; unexcused disobedience may be contempt, and a party receiving subpoenaed documents must make them available for copying by other parties.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-045.1","t":"Interstate subpoenas","s":"This rule lets a party in a case pending in another state (including D.C., Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, a federally recognized Indian tribe, or a U.S. territory) obtain a New Mexico subpoena for a deposition, documents, electronically stored information, things, or premises inspection by submitting the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court where discovery is to occur, which is not an appearance in New Mexico courts. The clerk promptly issues a subpoena incorporating the foreign subpoena’s terms and listing the names, addresses, and telephone numbers of all counsel of record and unrepresented parties. Service and compliance follow Rule 1-045, and motions for protective orders or to enforce, quash, or modify go to the court in the district where discovery is conducted under New Mexico law.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-046","t":"Preserving questions for review","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party preserves an issue by telling the court, when the ruling is made or sought, the action desired or the objection and its grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of objection. A motion for new trial is not needed to preserve errors brought to the trial court’s attention, and the rule applies to both jury and bench trials.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-047","t":"Jurors","s":"The court may let the parties or their attorneys examine prospective jurors or may conduct the examination itself, allowing supplemental questions as it deems proper. The court may seat up to 6 alternate jurors, who replace regular jurors in order before deliberations and are discharged when the jury retires; each side gets 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1 or 2 alternates, 2 for 3 or 4, and 3 for 5 or 6, usable only against alternates. Before voir dire each prospective juror completes Supreme Court-approved qualification and questionnaire forms, which are confidential unless unsealed under Rule 1-079; the court must destroy its copies 90 days after the juror’s term ends and attorneys, parties, and others within 120 days after final disposition (unless a court order allows longer), and attorneys and parties must file a sworn certification of compliance by the destruction deadline. The court may order supplemental questionnaires, normally at the requesting party’s actual cost, subject to the same protections.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-048","t":"Juries of fewer than twelve; stipulation","s":"Notwithstanding Rule 1-038, the parties may stipulate to a jury of any number fewer than twelve or that a stated majority of jurors’ verdict or finding will be the jury’s verdict or finding.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-049","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"The court may require a special verdict of written findings on each fact issue, and any issue omitted without a party’s demand before the jury retires is waived as to jury trial and may be found by the court or deemed found consistently with the judgment. The court may instead submit a general verdict with written interrogatories: consistent answers and verdict yield judgment; answers consistent with each other but inconsistent with the verdict allow judgment on the answers, further deliberation, or a new trial; and answers inconsistent with each other and the verdict require further deliberation or a new trial.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-050","t":"Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials; alternative motion for new trial; conditional rulings","s":"During a jury trial, after a party has been fully heard on an issue, the court may grant judgment as a matter of law if a reasonable jury would lack a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party; the motion may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and the supporting law and facts. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 30 days after entry of judgment, or, for a jury issue not decided by verdict, 30 days after the jury’s discharge (unlike the 28 days under FRCP 50), and may be joined with or alternate to a new trial motion under Rule 1-059, which by a party against whom judgment as a matter of law is entered must be filed within 30 days after entry of judgment. If the renewed motion is granted, the court must conditionally rule on any new trial motion and state its grounds, and an appellee who won on the motion may assert grounds for a new trial if the appellate court finds the denial erroneous.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-051","t":"Instructions to juries","s":"The judge must instruct the jury in the language of the Uniform Jury Instructions (UJI Civil) unless the parties waive instructions, using UJI Civil wherever applicable unless the court finds and states on the record why it is erroneous or improper, and must follow UJI directions not to instruct on a subject unless it states reasons otherwise; non-UJI instructions must be brief, impartial, and free of hypothesized facts. After the jury is sworn and before opening statements, the court must read applicable portions of UJI 13-106, which are not reread at the close but are sent to the jury room. Counsel must confer in good faith and prepare a single agreed set of written instructions, with an original, copies for parties, and 1 filing copy marked “given” or “refused,” each noting the UJI number or “Not in UJI Civil” (with no notations on the jury-room set), and unless waived the court charges the jury in writing before closing argument. To preserve error, a party must object to a given instruction or tender a correct instruction before the jury retires, and preserved instruction rulings are reviewable on appeal.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-052","t":"Nonjury trials; findings and conclusions","s":"In bench trials or trials with an advisory jury, the court enters findings of fact and conclusions of law only on a party’s timely request (unlike FRCP 52, which requires them in every such case), and none are needed on motions under Rules 1-012, 1-050, 1-056, or others except as provided in Rule 1-041(B). Unless the court orders otherwise, a party requests findings by filing proposed findings and conclusions no later than 10 days after the court announces its decision, and a party who filed them before trial may amend, supplement, or withdraw them within 10 days after the announcement. On motion filed not later than 30 days after entry of judgment, the court may amend or add findings and conclusions and amend the judgment.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-053","t":"Masters","s":"A court may appoint a special master (including a referee, auditor, or examiner), with compensation fixed by the court and enforceable by writ of execution, but reference is the exception: in jury cases only when issues are complicated, and in nonjury cases only for accounts, difficult damage computations, or an exceptional condition. Unless the order provides otherwise, the master must set a first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference, may compel evidence, rule on admissibility, administer oaths, use Rule 1-045 subpoenas, and prescribe accounting forms, and then files a report with the transcript and exhibits, with the clerk mailing notice of filing. In nonjury cases the court accepts the master’s findings unless clearly erroneous, parties may serve written objections within 10 days after service of notice of filing, and the court may adopt, modify, reject, take more evidence, or recommit; in jury cases the findings are admissible evidence, and stipulated-final findings leave only questions of law. The court may appoint a successor master who continues the proceedings with the original master’s powers.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-053.1","t":"Domestic violence special commissioners; duties","s":"Domestic violence special commissioners are at-will employees who must be New Mexico-licensed lawyers with at least 3 years of practice and knowledge of domestic relations and domestic violence, and who review petitions and motions under the Family Violence Protection Act, conduct on-the-record interviews and hearings, and prepare recommendations that take effect only when adopted by the district court; a commissioner may be removed for good cause. Recommendations on ex parte temporary orders go promptly to the judge for immediate review, and an ex parte order signed by the court stays in effect under Section 40-13-4 NMSA 1978 until the final order. Final recommendations are served with notice that specific objections may be filed within 14 days after service, identifying the objected portions, a summary of the evidence, disputed findings, and legal errors; the judge sets aside the decision only if arbitrary, capricious, an abuse of discretion, unsupported by substantial evidence, or contrary to law, conducts an on-the-record review (no in-person hearing required) of specific objections, may generally deny nonspecific ones, and enters a final order. Full-time commissioners may not practice law, part-time ones may if consistent with their duties, and all must follow the Code of Judicial Conduct.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-053.2","t":"Domestic relations hearing officers; duties","s":"Domestic relations hearing officers are at-will employees with the qualifications of a child support hearing officer under Section 40-4B-4 NMSA 1978 who may review indigency petitions, hear pre- and post-decree petitions and motions, act as child support hearing officers and domestic violence special commissioners, assist with the Domestic Relations Mediation Act, and prepare recommendations that take effect only when adopted by the court; they may be removed for good cause. Within 30 days after the proceedings conclude, the hearing officer files recommendations with proposed findings and conclusions and serves them with notice that specific objections may be filed within 14 days after service, identifying the objected portions, a summary of the evidence, disputed findings, and legal errors. The judge sets aside the decision only if arbitrary, capricious, an abuse of discretion, unsupported by substantial evidence, or contrary to law, reviews the record on specific objections without needing an in-person hearing, may generally deny nonspecific objections, and enters a final order, and this rule controls over any contrary Child Support Hearing Officer Act provision. Full-time hearing officers may not practice law, part-time ones may if consistent with their duties, and all must follow the Code of Judicial Conduct.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-053.3","t":"Guardians ad litem; domestic relations appointments","s":"In contested custody proceedings under Chapter 40 NMSA 1978, the court may appoint a guardian ad litem as a “best interests attorney” and arm of the court, by an order substantially in Form 4-402 specifying the role, duties, reasons, duration, retainer, hourly rate, monthly itemized billing, and fee allocation, and requiring the parties and children over 14 to sign requested releases; the court considers 17 listed factors and may not delegate the ultimate best-interests decision unless the parties agreed to arbitrate under Section 40-4-7.2 NMSA 1978. The guardian ad litem must interview a child aged 6 or older face-to-face outside the presence of parties and counsel (optional under 6), interview the parties and the child’s therapist, and serve a written report and separate recommendations on all parties at least 11 days before filing the recommendations, with notice of deadlines and the consequences of not objecting. Parties may submit a stipulated order adopting the recommendations, or file specific objections and a hearing request, within 11 days after filing; failure to object timely is a waiver and the court adopts the recommendations without a hearing, and objections to emergency recommendations get a hearing within 20 days. Child-guardian ad litem communications are privileged under Rule 11-503, other file materials are confidential trial preparation materials, and only the guardian ad litem may claim or make limited waivers of the privilege.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-054","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"A judgment includes a decree and any appealable order, and in multi-claim or multi-party cases the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express finding of no just reason for delay; default judgments may not differ in kind from or exceed the demand, while other judgments grant the relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded. Costs other than attorney fees go to the prevailing party unless the court directs otherwise (against the state only as the law permits), and the rule lists recoverable costs (filing and service fees, jury fees, approved transcripts, depositions used at trial or in summary judgment or reasonably necessary, witness travel and per diem, expert fees under Section 38-6-4(B) NMSA 1978 or if reasonably necessary, translator, exhibit, certification, and interpreter fees) and generally nonrecoverable ones (copying, telephone, fax, courier, attorney travel, paralegal and office expenses, and legal research). The prevailing party must file an itemized cost bill with proof of service within 15 days after filing of the final judgment or waives costs, and absent objections within 10 days after service the clerk taxes allowable costs. Attorney fee claims (other than fees proved as damages or sanctions) are made by motion filed and served no later than 15 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order provides otherwise, stating the judgment, grounds, amount, and basis.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-054.1","t":"Judgments and orders; time limit","s":"Notwithstanding Section 39-1-1 NMSA 1978, the court must enter a judgment or order within 60 days after submission, meaning the time the court takes the matter under advisement.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-054.2","t":"Judgments in residential foreclosure actions; certification concerning the absence of loss mitigation negotiations required; definition","s":"Before a district court enters judgment in a residential foreclosure, the plaintiff must file a certification, substantially in Form 4-712, concerning the absence of loss mitigation negotiations with the borrower; the rule does not apply to lien foreclosures. “Residential” means property designed principally for occupancy by 1 to 4 families and occupied by the defendant as the defendant’s principal residence.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-055","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters the default of a party who has failed to plead or otherwise defend when shown by affidavit or otherwise, but default judgment must always be sought from the court, cannot be entered against a minor or incompetent person without an appearing representative, and requires written notice at least 3 days before the hearing to a defaulting party who has appeared (an appearance with disclaimer of interest does not trigger notice). In cases under Rule 1-009(J), the court must first find a stated claim and compliance with Rules 1-009(J) and 1-017(E) and substantial compliance with Form 4-226 or 4-226A, and it may hold hearings or references on damages while preserving jury rights. The court may set aside an entry of default for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 1-060; the rule applies to all claimants, is subject to Rule 1-054(C), and bars default judgment against the state or its officers or agencies, on a negotiable instrument unless the original is filed and merged with the judgment, or for unliquidated damages unless proved by evidence satisfactory to the court.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-056","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant or defending party may move for summary judgment on all or part of a claim, with or without affidavits, and it is granted if the record shows no genuine issue of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law (liability alone may be decided), with the order specifying the grounds when alternative grounds are presented; motions must be filed a reasonable time before trial. The movant files a memorandum with numbered material facts citing the record, the opponent must respond within 15 days after service of the motion with numbered disputed facts citing the record and the movant’s fact numbers, and the movant may reply within 15 days after service of the response; facts not specifically controverted are deemed admitted. Affidavits must be on personal knowledge with admissible facts, the opponent may not rest on its pleadings, the court may grant a continuance when facts are unavailable, and for affidavits presented in bad faith or solely for delay the court must order the offending party to pay the resulting reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees, and offenders may be held in contempt. Effective December 31, 2026 (for cases pending or filed on or after that date), Paragraph G is amended to cover affidavits, declarations, and Rule 1-011(B) unsworn affirmations submitted in bad faith or for delay, makes the expense award discretionary and only after notice and a reasonable opportunity to respond, and adds other appropriate sanctions, with the rest of the rule restyled in gender-neutral language.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-057","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 1-038 and 1-039, the existence of another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing. When the State of New Mexico is a party, the summons and complaint must be personally served on both the governor and the attorney general, and the state must answer or plead and serve a copy within 20 days after service on the later-served of the two.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-058","t":"Orders and judgments; preparation and entry","s":"When the court announces a decision, it either allows counsel a reasonable time it fixes to submit a proposed order or judgment, designates counsel to prepare it by a set time, or prepares its own; if no satisfactory form is submitted in time, the court takes steps to have one entered promptly. Counsel must have a reasonable opportunity to examine and object before the court signs, and filing in the clerk’s office after signing constitutes entry.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-059","t":"New trials; motions directed against the judgment","s":"A new trial may be granted on all or some issues for any traditionally recognized reason after a jury trial, and after a bench trial the court may reopen the judgment, take more testimony, revise findings and conclusions, and enter a new judgment. A motion for new trial, and a motion to alter, amend, or reconsider a final judgment, must be filed not later than 30 days after entry of judgment (unlike the 28 days under FRCP 59); supporting affidavits are served with the motion, opposing affidavits within 15 days after service, extendable by up to 20 more days by the court for good cause or by written stipulation, and reply affidavits are allowed with permission. The court may order a new trial on its own initiative not later than 10 days after entry of judgment, or grant a timely motion on an unstated ground after notice and an opportunity to be heard, and must specify the grounds.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-060","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time on the court’s own initiative or on motion, but once an appeal is docketed only with leave of the appellate court. On motion the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, (3) fraud or misconduct of an adverse party, (4) a void judgment, (5) satisfaction, reversal of a prior judgment, or inequity of prospective application, or (6) any other reason, expressly including a consumer-debt plaintiff’s failure to comply with Rules 1-009(J) and 1-017(E) and Forms 4-226/4-226A. The motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for reasons (1)–(3) not more than 1 year after entry; it does not affect finality, independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief remain available, and the old writs (coram nobis, audita querela, bills of review) are abolished.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-061","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error or defect by the court or parties is ground for a new trial or for disturbing a verdict, judgment, or order unless refusing to act appears inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard at every stage any error that does not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-062","t":"Stay of proceeding to enforce a judgment","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, execution may issue as soon as judgment is entered (unlike FRCP 62(a), the text provides no automatic waiting period), and injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed pending appeal; the court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions under Rules 1-050, 1-052(D), 1-059, or 1-060 and may suspend, modify, or grant an injunction pending appeal on bond terms. An appellant obtains a stay by a supersedeas bond, given at or after filing the notice of appeal and effective when approved by the district court, covering the unsatisfied money judgment plus costs, interest, and delay damages; each personal surety must show net worth at least double the bond amount, personal-surety bonds need notice to the appellee, and the court considers collateral in its custody. Appeals by the state, its agencies, political subdivisions, or municipalities operate as an automatic stay; the court fixes bonds for fiduciaries, applies the same stay rules to writs of error (time running from allowance of the writ), and may stay a Rule 1-054(B) partial judgment pending later judgments; stays in election contests, mandamus, removal, quo warranto, and prohibition are discretionary.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-063","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"If a judge cannot continue a commenced trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and determining the case can be completed without prejudice to the parties. The successor judge may recall any witness.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-064","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"From the start of and throughout an action, all state-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available, under the circumstances and in the manner state law provides.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-065","t":"Writs issued by district courts","s":"The clerk may issue writs of execution, writs of possession under Section 42-4-12, and writs of attachment on land without judicial approval; all other writs (apart from those under Rules 1-065.1 and 1-065.2) require the judge’s express written approval endorsed on the writ and are sought by verified petition stating jurisdiction, standing, real parties in interest, grounds, and relief. The writ replaces the summons, must set a response or hearing date not earlier than 7 days after issuance unless the court finds unreasonable loss or hardship, and no peremptory writ may issue without a hearing set within 10 days of issuance. Prejudgment seizure or peremptory writs without notice require an affidavit of immediate irreparable injury, notice of exemption rights for natural persons, an attorney’s certification of notice efforts, and security (waivable for good cause); when the response or hearing date is less than 30 days after service, all defenses must be in one pleading and mail service is complete 3 days after mailing, and personal-property exemptions under Sections 42-10-1 to 42-10-7 apply to attachment.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-065.1","t":"Writs of execution","s":"Unless the judgment is stayed, the clerk issues a writ of execution on timely application at any time against a non-natural-person debtor, and against a natural person only on a certificate or affidavit that the exemption notice was served and no claim filed, an order finding the property not exempt, or the debtor’s written waiver describing the property. The creditor must serve a natural-person debtor with a notice of right to claim exemptions at least 10 days before seizure; the debtor has 10 days after service to file a claim (failure waives exemptions, except that for actions filed on or after July 1, 2023 the first $2,400 in a depository or investment account need not be claimed, and unemployment compensation protections are not waived); the creditor has 10 days after service of the claim to dispute it, and the court must hold a hearing within 10 days after the dispute is filed. Medical-debt judgments require an application complying with Form 4-805C, the sheriff must serve the writ within 60 days of issuance (with later writs available on request), and Supreme Court-approved forms are used.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-065.2","t":"Garnishment","s":"After judgment and on timely application the clerk issues a writ of garnishment (medical-debt judgments require an application complying with Form 4-805B), which the creditor serves on the garnishee under Rule 1-004 together with the application, exemption notice and claim form, and answer form, while mailing a copy to the debtor and debtor’s counsel and filing a certificate of service. The garnishee must answer within 20 days of service and, by the fourth business day after service, deliver copies of the forms to the debtor; corporations may answer through an officer, director, or general manager but must otherwise appear through counsel, and the court awards the garnishee reasonable attorney fees and costs. A natural-person debtor gets wage exemptions by law and may claim other exemptions within 10 days after the garnishee serves the notice (failure waives non-wage exemptions, except the first $2,400 in a depository or investment account for actions filed on or after July 1, 2023, and unemployment compensation protections); the creditor has 10 days to dispute or the claim is granted, a hearing must be held within 10 days after the dispute is filed, and no judgment on the writ enters until that hearing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-066","t":"Injunctions and receivers","s":"No preliminary injunction or receiver may issue without notice, and the court may consolidate the merits trial with the preliminary-injunction hearing, preserving jury rights. A temporary restraining order without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and an attorney’s certification of notice efforts; it must state date and hour, define the injury, and expire within a court-fixed time not exceeding 10 days (unlike FRCP 65’s 14 days), extendable once for a like period for good cause or longer by consent, and if the adverse party disqualifies the judge it is deemed extended until 10 days after designation of a new judge. The preliminary-injunction hearing gets precedence, the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice, security is required for any restraining order, injunction, or receivership unless waived for good cause recited in the order, and sureties submit to the court’s jurisdiction with the clerk as their agent for enforcement by motion.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-067","t":"Deposit in court","s":"When relief sought includes a sum of money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice to every other party and with leave of court, deposit all or part of the sum or thing with the court.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-068","t":"Offer of settlement","s":"Unlike FRCP 68, any party, including a claimant, may serve an offer to allow judgment on specified terms at any time more than 10 days before trial, but a claimant may not make an offer until 120 days after the defending party files a responsive pleading; the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party may file the offer and acceptance for entry of judgment, and an unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and is inadmissible except on costs. If a claimant’s rejected offer is beaten (the claimant’s judgment is more favorable than the offer), the defending party pays the claimant’s costs, excluding attorney fees, including double the costs incurred after the offer; if a defending party’s rejected offer is not beaten, the claimant pays the defending party’s post-offer costs, excluding attorney fees, and cannot recover its own costs incurred thereafter. A rejected offer does not bar later offers, and after liability is determined an offer served within a reasonable time not less than 10 days before the damages hearing has the same effect. The rule does not apply to domestic relations actions, and a claimant entitled to both double costs and statutory interest receives either double costs or interest plus Rule 1-054(D)(2) costs, not both.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-069","t":"Judgment; supplementary proceedings","s":"On request of the judgment creditor or a successor, the clerk issues a subpoena requiring any person with knowledge useful for enforcement, including the debtor, to appear before the district court for examination, served at least 3 days before the examination. In lieu of the examination, the creditor may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, by any method in these rules.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-070","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a judgment-directed act (such as conveying land or delivering deeds) within the time specified, the court may have it done by an appointee at the disobedient party’s cost with the same effect, the clerk shall issue a writ of attachment or sequestration on application, and the court may hold the party in contempt. For property within the state the court may instead enter a judgment divesting and vesting title, which operates as a conveyance, and a party awarded possession is entitled to a writ of execution or assistance from the clerk.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071.1","t":"Statutory stream system adjudication suits; service and joinder of water rights claimants; responses","s":"In stream system adjudications under Section 72-4-17, the court may order the plaintiff to join water rights claimants by stream-system subsections, which the plaintiff does by serving a court-approved proposed consent order or other document conspicuously notifying the claimant of the duty to respond. Service follows Rule 1-004 except that the plaintiff issues and signs the summons, and service or a waiver of service joins the claimant as a defendant without further order. Claimants must respond within court-set deadlines; a claimant who fails to respond to a proposed consent order may suffer a Rule 1-055 default adjudicating rights as proposed, and for other documents the default adopts the Section 72-4-16 hydrographic survey unless the court for good cause orders otherwise.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071.2","t":"Statutory stream system adjudication suits; stream system issue and expedited inter se proceedings","s":"Before notice of commencement of inter se proceedings, any party (or the court sua sponte) may seek designation of a “stream system issue,” which the court must designate after a hearing if its resolution could directly affect all or a significant number of claimants or if a non-binding resolution would risk inconsistent decisions or practically preclude similarly situated claimants. The plaintiff or any claimant may also seek an expedited inter se proceeding resolving one claim against the plaintiff and all other claimants, which the court may order after a hearing if it promotes efficiency, weighing injury to the claimant, injury to opponents, and the expense and delay of not proceeding. Notwithstanding Rule 1-004, notice (normally given by the movant) goes by first-class mail to all known claimants in the designated sections, identified from hydrographic surveys and assessor, state engineer, and water association records, and by a court-approved method to unknown claimants; participants must file a notice of intent within the court-set time, and the resulting order binds all claimants whether or not joined, participating, or actually notified, provided notice complied with the rule.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071.3","t":"Statutory stream system adjudication suits; annual joint working session","s":"Thirty (30) days before the end of each fiscal year, the judges, special masters, the state, and other parties in each stream adjudication court must set a joint working session on common issues and resource needs, and state judges must invite federal adjudication judges and special masters. The state must file a report of its suggested priorities and resource analysis for each pending state adjudication 30 days before the session.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071.4","t":"Statutory stream system adjudication suits; ex parte contacts; general problems of administration","s":"Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 21-209(A) applies to stream adjudications, except that judges, special masters, and their staff may communicate with the plaintiff on matters not addressing the merits that concern general administration and management of a pending or impending adjudication or accurate reporting of water rights claims in court records.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-071.5","t":"Statutory stream system adjudication suits; excusal or recusal of a water judge","s":"Water judges, including pro tem and sitting judges assigned to stream adjudications, are designated by the chief justice; under Rule 1-088(E) they cannot be peremptorily excused. If a water judge is excused for cause or recuses, the chief justice reassigns the matter to another designated water judge.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-072","t":"Appeal from magistrate and metropolitan courts in trial de novo cases","s":"An aggrieved party in a magistrate or metropolitan court civil action appeals to the district court of that county by filing a notice of appeal (with the lower-court judgment attached and proof of Rule 1-005 service) within 15 days after the judgment or final order is filed, with any other party having 10 days after service of the first notice or the original period, whichever is later; the 3-day mailing period does not apply, premature notices are timely, endorsed copies and the docket-fee receipt must be promptly filed in the lower court, and no docket fee or cost is charged to the state, its subdivisions, or Educational Assistance Act nonprofits. A jury demand under Rule 1-038 must be filed with the notice of appeal but no later than 30 days after service of the notice, or 10 days after the last pleading if additional pleadings are filed; the lower court files the record (papers, judgment, exhibits, any transcript or audio duplicate with index log) within 15 days after the endorsed notice is filed there; and an amended complaint may be filed within 30 days after service of the notice of appeal, with a response due within 30 days. Stays require a supersedeas bond posted in the lower court (appeals by the state, its agencies, subdivisions, or municipalities stay automatically), and the district court may review supersedeas rulings and allow more time to post a bond. A reconsideration motion is due within 10 days after the final order (no 3-day mailing period, no response unless requested), district court opinions are unpublished and non-precedential, the mandate issues at the latest of 30 days after the disposing order, 30 days after disposition of rehearing, or final disposition of any further appeal, and cases within lower-court jurisdiction are remanded for enforcement.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-073","t":"Withdrawn","s":"Withdrawn; no current rule text.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-074","t":"Administrative appeals; statutory review by district court of administrative decisions or orders","s":"This rule governs appeals to the district court from state or local agencies where a statute grants review (it creates no right to appeal and excludes non-statutory review under Rule 1-075, Human Rights Act appeals, unemployment compensation appeals, and Article XVI, Section 5 water-rights matters). Unless a statute or local ordinance sets another time (and a conflicting statutory period controls), the notice of appeal, with the agency decision attached, must be filed within 30 days after the agency’s final decision, other parties may appeal within 10 days after service of the first notice or the original period, whichever is later, the 3-day mailing period does not apply, and premature notices are timely; the appellant must serve all parties, file a certificate of arrangements for the record, and file an endorsed copy with the agency, and no docket fee or cost is charged to state agencies or political subdivisions. Unless a law provides otherwise, the agency files the paginated record within 30 days after the notice is filed with it (parties may stipulate to a partial record within 5 days); the appellant’s statement of appellate issues is due 30 days after service of notice of filing of the record, the response 30 days later, and any reply 15 days after that, with limits of 25 pages for the statement, 25 for the response, and 10 for the reply, unsupported substantial-evidence challenges deemed waived, and unattacked findings deemed conclusive. Oral argument must be requested before all response times expire (otherwise the appellant files a notice of completion of briefing); listed defenses may be raised by motion without staying proceedings; a stay motion must show a prior agency denial or impracticability plus irreparable injury, likely success, no substantial harm to others, and no harm to the public interest, and may require a bond; review asks whether the agency acted fraudulently, arbitrarily, or capriciously, lacked substantial evidence on the whole record, exceeded its authority, or otherwise violated law; certification to the Court of Appeals may be sought by motion within 30 days of filing the notice (15 days for response) on substantial-public-interest grounds; a rehearing motion is due within 10 days after the final order (no 3-day mailing period); further review is under Rule 12-505; and failure to file a statement of issues may result in dismissal, though timely appeals are not dismissed for technical violations.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-075","t":"Constitutional review by district court of administrative decisions and orders","s":"Where no statutory right of appeal or review exists, an aggrieved party may seek review of a final state or local agency decision by filing a petition for writ of certiorari (stating jurisdiction, the agency proceedings, the parties, entitlement to relief, and relief sought, with the decision attached) within 30 days after the final decision; other parties may petition within 10 days after service of the first petition or the original period, whichever is later, the 3-day mailing period does not apply, and premature petitions are timely. The rule creates no right to review and excludes Article XVI, Section 5 water-rights matters; the petitioner must serve all parties, file proof of service and a certificate of transcript arrangements, and file an endorsed copy with the agency, and no docket fee is charged to state agencies or political subdivisions. The court issues the writ if the procedural requirements are met and the petition makes a prima facie showing of jurisdiction, entitlement to relief, and no right of appeal; the writ does not automatically stay agency proceedings and is served by delivery or certified mail, and the agency files the record within 30 days after service of the writ or as ordered (partial-record stipulations within 5 days after the petition). Briefing, page limits (25/25/10), deadlines (30/30/15 days), oral argument, motions, stay standards, standard of review, certification (motion within 30 days of the petition, 15 days for response), decision, 10-day rehearing motion, and sanctions track Rule 1-074.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-076","t":"Appeals from Human Rights Commission","s":"De novo appeals from the Human Rights Commission are taken by filing a notice of appeal in the form of a civil complaint, by any person aggrieved by a commission order or, after the director serves a waiver of the right to hearing, by the complainant; the complaint may add new issues and new parties (served under Rule 1-004). The appeal must be filed within 90 days after service of the commission’s order or of the director’s or complainant’s notice of waiver of hearing, other parties may file a cross-complaint within 10 days after service of the notice of appeal or within the original period, whichever is later, the 3-day mailing period does not apply, and premature notices are timely; no docket fee or cost is charged to state agencies or political subdivisions. Each party designates transcript portions within 10 days after service of the notice of appeal and the Human Rights Division files them within 30 days after receiving the designations; the ordinary civil rules and Rule 1-038 jury demands apply, a reconsideration motion is due within 10 days after the final order (no 3-day mailing period, no response unless requested), and the court may stay the commission’s order after notice and hearing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-077","t":"Appeals pursuant to Unemployment Compensation Law","s":"Appeals from final decisions of the board of review or the secretary of the Department of Workforce Solutions under Section 51-1-8 are taken by a petition for writ of certiorari, filed in the county where the petitioner resides (another county’s court may hear it if equity and due process require), within 30 days from the final decision, with no 3-day mailing period. The petition, served on the Department’s Office of General Counsel, the opposing employer or employee, and all other parties, must state the issues, a concise summary of proceedings showing preservation, and the precise relief sought; benefit claimants may not be charged any court fees, and no bond is required. The court’s writ directs the Department to file the record, including a certified transcript or audio duplicate, within 20 days of service; the appeal is heard summarily with precedence over all other civil cases on the agency record, and the court may reverse only for fraudulent, arbitrary, or capricious action, lack of substantial evidence on the whole record, or action outside the agency’s authority. A reconsideration motion is due within 10 days after the final order (no 3-day mailing period), and further review is under Rule 12-505.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-077.1","t":"Expungement","s":"Expungement under the Criminal Record Expungement Act (Sections 29-3A-1 to -9, except automatic cannabis expungement under Section 29-3A-8) is commenced by a civil petition on mandatory forms (Forms 4-951 through 4-960.3), filed in the county where charges originated, the arrest occurred, or the conviction was entered depending on the type (identity theft, no conviction, or conviction); one petition may cover any number of arrests, charges, and convictions in a single judicial district, and petitions for release without conviction are filed under seal under Rule 1-079 unless combined with a conviction petition. Petitions for release without conviction must be served by first-class mail on the district attorney and the Department of Public Safety, and conviction petitions also on the arresting agency; the court may allow 60 days to amend a deficient petition before dismissing without prejudice, and served parties must file specific objections or a notice of non-objection within 60 days of service (providing the FBI rap sheet free of charge if objecting on that basis). After the objection period the petitioner files a notice of completion of briefing with an affirmation, and parties get 20 days to object to newly disclosed arrests, charges, or convictions; the petitioner bears the burden of proof, no merits hearing is set before the notice of completion, unopposed identity-theft and no-conviction petitions may be decided without a hearing, and conviction petitions require a hearing (remote participation by notice, without motion). After a hearing the court must rule within 60 days; an expungement order must allow at least 60 days for compliance, require expungement of the civil proceeding itself, not expunge court records earlier than 30 days after entry, and be delivered to all relevant law enforcement agencies and courts (including any appellate court with related records).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-077.2","t":"Water court structure and procedure","s":"The state is divided into three water regions (WR1: First, Second, Eleventh, and Thirteenth Judicial Districts; WR2: Third, Sixth, and Seventh; WR3: Fourth, Fifth, Eighth, Ninth, Tenth, and Twelfth), and the Supreme Court appoints 5 district judges as water judges (one in WR1, two in WR2, one in WR3, and one alternate), with currently designated judges continuing until replaced. Water judges cannot be excused under Rule 1-088.1 or Section 38-3-9 and may be disqualified only under Article VI, Section 18 of the New Mexico Constitution; they hear cases under Sections 72-1-1 to 72-20-103 and 73-1-1 to 73-20-19, State Engineer administrative appeals under Section 72-7-1, and significant-impairment cases under Section 72-12-28, while judges presiding over listed stream adjudications continue until further order. Water cases are filed in the county where the waters or point of diversion lie, routed to the regional water clerk, keep their original case number with a “(WATER REGION X)” caption suffix, receive the same priority as juvenile and children’s court matters, and water judges must attend annual water-law training and earn at least 7 water-law CLE credits each year; the rule was provisionally adopted effective October 1, 2025.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-078","t":"Motion day","s":"Unless local conditions make it impracticable, each district court must set regular, sufficiently frequent times and places to hear motions requiring notice and hearing, but the judge may at any time, on such notice as the judge considers reasonable, make orders advancing, conducting, and hearing actions.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-079","t":"Public inspection and sealing of court records","s":"Court records (filed or lodged material plus the register of actions and docket entries) are presumptively public unless sealed by order or protected by the rule, and no one may knowingly file material taken from a sealed, conditionally sealed, or pending-motion record. All records are automatically sealed without motion in 11 listed proceeding types, including adoption, Section 24-1-15 detention, Section 24-2B-5.1 testing, Adult Protective Services, Mental Health and Developmental Disabilities Code, deposited unprobated wills, guardianship and conservatorship (per Rule 1-079.1), firearm-disability removal, Assisted Outpatient Treatment, and Section 29-3A-4 expungement (no conviction), subject to statutory disclosure and firearm reporting, though the docket number and case type remain public absent court order; protected personal identifier information (all but the last 4 digits of SSNs, taxpayer-ID, financial account, and driver’s license numbers, and all but the year of birth) should be omitted, may not appear on public court websites or courthouse displays, clerks need not screen for it, and requesters must provide name, address, phone, and identification. Otherwise sealing requires a motion by any party or member of the public, with the record lodged in a container labeled “CONDITIONALLY UNDER SEAL” and conditionally sealed pending ruling (returned if denied), and the court may not seal on stipulation alone but only by written findings of an overriding interest, a substantial probability of prejudice, narrow tailoring, and no less restrictive means; the order must limit sealing to necessary parts, address the order, register, and docket entries, specify who has access, an expiration date or event (or until further order), and who gets notice of motions to unseal. Records sealed below or in agency proceedings stay sealed on appeal, unsealing requires a motion served on persons named in the sealing order and decided under the same standards, and knowing disclosure of sealed or lodged material may be punished as contempt or otherwise sanctioned.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-079.1","t":"Public inspection and sealing of court records; guardianship and conservatorship proceedings","s":"This rule supplements Rule 1-079’s automatic sealing of guardianship (Chapter 45, Article 5, Part 3) and conservatorship (Part 4) proceedings, with all other access matters, including motions to seal or unseal, still governed by Rule 1-079. All records are automatically sealed subject to firearm reporting under Section 34-9-19, except that the register of actions and docket entries remain public without medical or psychological information; persons named in the petition may access the appointment order and all records filed before it, while later records are limited to the protected person, the guardian or conservator, and persons the court authorizes under listed statutes or Rules 1-140 and 1-141. Reports by health care professionals, visitors, guardians ad litem, and guardians or conservators are limited to the protected person, petitioner, visitor, guardian ad litem, attorneys of record, an agent under a power of attorney (unless the court orders otherwise), and others the court designates.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-080","t":"Stenographer; stenographic report or transcript as evidence","s":"A master may have evidence taken stenographically and appoint a stenographer whose fees the court fixes and may tax as costs, and the court may order recording by other means with safeguards, though a party may still arrange a stenographic transcript at its own expense. Stenographically reported testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-081","t":"Remand to district court from federal court","s":"After a case removed to federal court is remanded, no default judgment may be entered until 10 days after service on the defendants of notice that the remand order has been filed, and within that time defendants may move or plead as if the case had not been removed.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-082","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or the venue of actions.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-083","t":"Withdrawn","s":"Withdrawn; no current rule text.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-084","t":"Bankruptcy proceedings; stay","s":"A party must file written notice of any bankruptcy court stay that may affect the pending action upon becoming aware of it, and likewise must file written notice of the stay’s termination or modification upon becoming aware of it.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-085","t":"Judgments or orders on mandate","s":"Within 30 days after the appellate court sends its mandate to the district court, the prevailing party on appeal must present a proposed judgment or order containing the appellate court’s specific directions or, if necessary, request a hearing. The proposed judgment or order must be served on all parties.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-086","t":"Repealing and saving clause","s":"Existing Supreme Court rules of pleading, practice, and procedure for courts other than the Supreme Court that are not contained in these rules remain in force unless superseded, modified, or repealed by these rules.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-087","t":"Contest of nomination or election","s":"Under Section 1-14-1, any unsuccessful candidate may contest a nomination or election by filing a verified complaint in the district court of the county where a party resides no later than 30 days after issuance of the certificate of nomination or election, with the general civil rules applying where not inconsistent. The contestee must file and serve a verified answer within 15 days after service, and any peremptory challenge to the judge under Section 38-3-9 and Rule 1-088.1 must be exercised by affidavit of disqualification on or before the answer deadline. Contests are advanced for hearing and decision, and either party may preserve ballots (Section 1-14-6), petition to impound ballots in precincts where the petitioner is a candidate (Sections 1-14-8 to 1-14-12), and apply for a recount or recheck (Sections 1-14-14 to 1-14-18).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-088","t":"Designation of judge","s":"The trial judge is designated when the complaint is filed under local rule, and when a judge is excused or recused the clerk randomly assigns a judge from another division, or, if all judges in the district are excused or recused, notifies the chief justice, who designates a judge, justice, or judge pro tempore. Civil cases by or against a judge or employee of the district are automatically assigned to a judge from another district under the chief justice’s procedures, and if all judges are ineligible when an emergency order or Rule 1-066 TRO is sought, the clerk immediately certifies the case to the Supreme Court for designation. A judge designated by the chief justice, and the successor who takes over that judge’s cases, may be excused only under Article VI, Section 18 of the New Mexico Constitution, not peremptorily.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-088.1","t":"Peremptory excusal of a district judge; recusal; procedure for exercising","s":"Each party may peremptorily excuse no more than one judge, and not after attending a hearing before or requesting any act of that judge other than free process or an indigency determination; aligned co-plaintiffs or co-defendants count as one party when they share a lawyer or firm, file joint pleadings, are spouses, parents, children, or siblings, are a business or organization with its owners, parents, subsidiaries, officers, directors, or major shareholders, or are a government agency with its subordinate units, though the assigned judge may allow separate excusals for parties with sufficiently diverse interests. A plaintiff must file the excusal within 10 days after service of notice of assignment of the first judge, and a defendant within 10 days after filing its first Rule 1-012 pleading or motion; any party may excuse within 10 days after the clerk serves notice of reassignment or completes publication of a mass reassignment (100 or more pending cases reassigned at once, noticed for 4 consecutive weeks on the State Bar website and in 2 consecutive Bar Bulletins, complete on the date of the second issue); on a motion to reopen a case reassigned since judgment, the movant has 10 days after filing the motion and service of the reassignment notice and the non-movant 10 days after service of the motion. Overriding these windows, no original or later-added party may excuse a judge more than 120 days after that judge was assigned to the case; the excusing party must serve notice on all parties, and the excused judge rules on any objection to timeliness or validity (raised by a party or the court), proceeding further only if the excusal is invalid. Excusals may not be used to hinder or delay, and the chief judge reports suspected misuse to the chief justice, who may bar the attorneys or their clients from filing excusals for a period; recusal for cause remains available, and a judge whose impartiality may reasonably be questioned must file a recusal, of which the clerk gives written notice to each party.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-089","t":"Entry of appearance; withdrawal or substitution of attorneys","s":"An attorney representing a party must file an entry of appearance (unless appointed by court order), and filing a signed pleading counts as one; an attorney with a limited appearance under Rule 16-102(C) must file a “Limited Entry of Appearance” describing the limitation and note the limitation and a service address for the party in every signature block. A court order is needed to withdraw after an unlimited appearance or before a limited representation is complete, and if withdrawal leaves a party unrepresented the order must advise that the party has 20 days to retain an attorney or be deemed to appear pro se; a limited-scope attorney who has finished the representation need only file and serve a notice of withdrawal or substitution. Attorneys of record remain subject to service for 90 days after final judgment, and after those 90 days further proceedings require service of process on the responding party under Rule 1-004.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-089.1","t":"Nonadmitted and nonresident counsel","s":"Lawyers licensed and in good standing in another state or country may, after complying with Rule 24-106, appear only in association with New Mexico counsel, who must attend all proceedings in person unless excused; the out-of-state lawyer files an affidavit of good standing and Rule 24-106 compliance with the first paper filed (or as soon as practicable after being retained) and is then deemed admitted without any separate motion or order. New Mexico counsel must sign the first motion or pleading, must have name and address on all later papers, and is deemed to sign every later pleading for Rule 1-011 purposes; the court may sanction noncompliance or other good cause, including by terminating the appearance. The court may require nonresident lawyers licensed in New Mexico to associate resident New Mexico counsel, and lawyers licensed elsewhere may take part in discovery arising from litigation pending in another state or territory without New Mexico counsel unless the court requires association in a specific proceeding.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-090","t":"Conduct of court proceedings","s":"Judicial proceedings exist to find the truth and should be conducted with dignity and decorum. Ceremonial (nonjudicial) proceedings held by judges in open court may be photographed or broadcast from the courtroom with the court’s permission and under its supervision.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-091","t":"Adopting procedural statutes","s":"Statutes on pleading, practice and procedure in effect when the 1933 act (L. 1933, ch. 84; Sections 38-1-1 and 38-1-2 NMSA 1978) took effect, and those enacted since, remain in force as rules of court except to the extent the Supreme Court has amended or vacated them or does so in the future.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-092","t":"Nonstenographic recording","s":"On its own motion or a party’s motion, the district court may order that all or part of the record be made by nonstenographic means; the order must designate the portions so recorded and the method of recording and preservation, and may include safeguards for accuracy, such as also using a court reporter.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-093","t":"Punitive contempt","s":"Punitive contempt arising in civil proceedings is governed by Rule 5-112 NMRA (the criminal-procedure contempt rule), as reinstated and amended for all cases pending or filed on or after October 9, 2025.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-094","t":"Clinical education; university of New Mexico school of law","s":"Students admitted to the UNM School of Law clinical program may advise clients, negotiate and appear in civil and criminal matters before state courts and agencies under the active supervision of a State Bar member designated by the dean, who must assign all matters, review all documents, sign all pleadings and be present at court appearances (though not during advising or negotiating). A student may appear in a given court only with the presiding judge’s written approval, in substantially the Supreme Court-approved form, filed in the court, and the law school reports annually to the Supreme Court. Eligible students are full-time students in good standing who have not graduated, have passed at least 30 semester hours (or equivalent), and meet the dean’s academic and moral standards.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-094.1","t":"Clinical education; out-of-state law school approved programs","s":"Students regularly enrolled at an ABA-accredited law school may practice in New Mexico courts and agencies in civil and criminal matters under the active supervision of a State Bar member admitted for at least 5 years, who must assign matters, review documents, sign pleadings and attend court appearances, and each student needs the presiding judge’s written approval, filed with the dean’s certificate. The dean’s certificate must state that the program meets ABA “field placement” standards, the student has passed at least 30 semester hours (or equivalent) and meets the school’s academic and moral standards, and the participation period, with certificates limited to terms of no more than 4 months. Before participating, the student must read and be familiar with the Rules of Professional Conduct and this rule.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-095","t":"Informal probate proceedings in probate court","s":"For informal probate proceedings filed in probate court, the probate court must notify the district court clerk in writing of the case style, filer and attorney, any personal representative appointed, and, on closing, send a copy of the full docket sheet; after that it must promptly report each later filing (with its type and date, and any change in the personal representative’s name or address). Certified copies of probate-court filings come from the probate clerk on payment of the legal fees, not the district court clerk, and a creditor filing a claim in district court under Section 45-3-804 while an informal probate is pending must pay the regular civil docket fee, with a copy of the claim sent to the probate court. Demands for notice filed in district court under Section 45-3-204 must be promptly forwarded to the probate clerk and mailed to the personal representative once that person is known.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-096","t":"Challenge of nominating petition","s":"A challenge to a nominating petition under the Primary Election Law must be filed as a complaint with a request for expedited hearing within 10 days after the last day to file the declaration of candidacy, served under Rule 1-004 on the proper filing officer, and immediately delivered both to the assigned judge and to the candidate in a manner reasonably calculated to give actual notice. Each challenged signature must be pleaded in a separate count (or grouped in one count if challenged on a single common ground) with its page and line, grounds and a copy of the petition, and duplicate-signature and qualification challenges require further exhibits and details (including whether the signer was a party member 10 days before the petition was filed, and the voter records searched, by whom and when); objections not pleaded this way within 10 days after the last filing day are conclusively waived. The plaintiff may peremptorily excuse the judge only on the day the complaint is filed (one collective excusal for multiple plaintiffs) and the candidate within 2 days after delivery, the court must hold a hearing and decide within 10 days after filing, and appeal is to the Supreme Court under Rule 12-603.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-096.1","t":"Review of election recall petitions","s":"Before circulating a petition to recall an elected county official under Article X, Section 9 of the New Mexico Constitution, the proponent must file a district court complaint attaching the proposed petition and stating the alleged malfeasance, misfeasance or oath violation; a challenge to a local school board member recall petition must be filed within 10 days after the county clerk decides whether enough signatures were submitted, stating any challenges to signatures, the clerk’s minimum-signature determination, or the sufficiency of the charges. Signature challenges must identify each signature by page and line with specific grounds, attach any other petition for duplicate-signature claims, and for registration challenges allege a diligent search and state the county, searchers, dates and name variations searched. The complaint and notice of hearing must be served immediately under Rule 1-004 on the county clerk, the official, and the recall proponent, the court must hold a hearing and decide within 10 days after filing, and appeal is to the Supreme Court under Rule 12-603.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-097","t":"Eminent domain; notice of presentation of petition; service","s":"A copy of an eminent domain petition and notice of when and where it will be presented must be served on each owner at least 10 days before the presentation date, either personally or, for an absent in-state owner, by leaving it at the owner’s usual abode with a resident over 15 years old; service may be made by any disinterested person over 18, proven by affidavit. For unknown, nonresident or unfound owners, notice must be published for 3 consecutive weeks before the hearing, the last publication at least 3 days before it, in a newspaper published in the county (or, if none, one of general circulation there). Personal service outside New Mexico at least 20 days before the presentation date is equivalent to publication.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-098","t":"Withdrawn","s":"This rule has been withdrawn; no text is in effect.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-099","t":"District court civil filing fees","s":"The docket fee prescribed by law is charged for docketing any civil cause, whether original, reopened, or on appeal or transfer from a court of limited jurisdiction, except that no fee is charged for papers filed within 90 days after final disposition, for a motion to correct a mistake in the judgment, order or record, or, if a docket fee was already paid or waived in the case, for a stipulated order or other request the clerk can act on (even if the judge must also act) or a motion to enforce a child support order. Miscellaneous fees are $1.50 for taking one person’s acknowledgment and affixing the seal, $.75 for each additional person at the same time, $.35 per typewritten folio for a single copy of records and for each additional copy ordered at the same time, $.35 per page for photographically reproduced copies, and $1.50 for a certificate and seal authenticating a paper as a true copy. Effective December 31, 2026, an added sentence provides that the cost of electronic records produced on physical media is governed by Rule 23-119 NMRA.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-100","t":"Form of papers","s":"Except exhibits and electronically filed papers under Rule 1-005.2, papers filed in district court must be legible, printed on one side of good-quality white 8½ x 11 inch paper with 1-inch left and right margins and 1½-inch top and bottom margins, consecutively page-numbered at the bottom, stapled at the upper left, and (except a cover page) in pica (10 pitch) type or 12-point typeface, double-spaced except quotations and footnotes. A space of at least 2½ by 2½ inches must be left in the upper right corner of the first page for the clerk’s stamp. Exhibit copies must be clear black images on white paper, one side per page, at original size or any smaller size not less than 8½ x 11 inches.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-101","t":"Reserved","s":"This rule number is reserved; no rule is in effect.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-102","t":"Deposit of litigant funds","s":"Litigant funds deposited with the court must be placed within 2 business days in one or more trust checking accounts at an FDIC-member bank, separate from general court funds, and invested under Section 34-6-36 (or, for Section 42A-1-19 deposits, in federal securities or federally insured accounts within the judicial district). Interest goes to the person entitled to the principal only for a single deposit of $25,000 or more held at least 30 days when the court so orders on the parties’ request and stipulation, or in eminent domain cases where the statute provides for investment at interest; before paying interest the clerk must obtain a completed Form W-9 and keep a record of the payee’s name, address, taxpayer ID and interest amount. All other deposits go into an administrative trust account, whose interest the clerk must remit to the state treasurer within 10 days after receiving each monthly statement.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-103","t":"Court interpreters","s":"A court interpreter must be appointed whenever a case participant cannot communicate in English well enough to participate fully, including on request or when the court observes the need; a party must give notice of its own need when filing its complaint, petition, answer or other responsive pleading, and of a witness’s need in writing on service of a notice of hearing (stating whether the proceeding will exceed 2 hours), and late notice may result in costs. The court must appoint a certified interpreter, falling back (after diligent efforts and consulting the Administrative Office of the Courts) to a justice system interpreter and then to a language access specialist or less qualified interpreter only on notice and on-record findings, never using a non-certified interpreter for a juror, and neither the judge nor a party’s attorney may serve as interpreter; parties may, with court approval, stipulate to a non-certified interpreter for non-evidentiary hearings, and a participant may waive an interpreter only knowingly, voluntarily and intelligently on the record. Spoken-language proceedings expected to last more than 2 hours require a team of 2 interpreters (shorter ones allow 1 interpreter with breaks about every 30 minutes), signed-language proceedings over 1 hour require 2 per participant, a single interpreter may substitute only if 2 cannot be found after contacting the AOC and 5-minute breaks are allowed about every 30 minutes; costs are paid from the Jury and Witness Fee Fund except that parties pay for their own attorney-client interpreters and for requested recordings, and a party who fails to cancel an unneeded interpreter may be charged the costs.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-104","t":"Courtroom closure","s":"All courtroom proceedings are open unless closed automatically (adoption hearings, communicable-disease detention, testing proceedings under Section 24-2B-5.1, and Uniform Parentage Act pretrial proceedings) or by written order, and the parties’ agreement alone is not enough to close a courtroom. A party or interested person must file and serve a motion to close at least 45 days before the proceeding unless the court waives the time for good cause; responses (to a motion or to the court’s own order to show cause) are due within 15 days after service and replies within 15 days after the response, unless the court sets different times, and any member of the public may respond any time before the required public hearing, for which requesting media and others get timely notice. A closure order must be written and find that closure is necessary to protect an overriding interest likely to be prejudiced, is narrowly tailored, and that reasonable alternatives were considered; in camera material not ordered disclosed is sealed and preserved for appeal.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-105","t":"Notice to statutory beneficiaries in wrongful death cases","s":"After a personal representative is appointed under the Wrongful Death Act, the personal representative must give notice under Rule 1-004 to all known or reasonably ascertainable statutory beneficiaries. The notice must give the personal representative’s name and the lawyer’s contact information, acknowledge the duty to act in all beneficiaries’ best interests, instruct beneficiaries to keep their contact information current, and state that beneficiaries will be told of trial settings, dismissals, settlements, verdicts and any proposed distribution, and of any hearing on a distribution dispute, which they may attend. Later notices are given under Rule 1-005.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-106","t":"Enforcement of mediated settlement agreement","s":"When parties settle by a mediated agreement requiring performance over time without a stipulated judgment, they file a stipulation of dismissal and a signed written agreement (unless they agree in writing to waive filing it, in which case they must keep and produce their own copies), and the court closes the case but keeps jurisdiction to reopen and enforce it. Within 5 years of the stipulation of dismissal, a party alleging noncompliance may file and serve a motion for judgment and statement of noncompliance with the agreement, and the clerk reopens the case without an additional filing fee; the other party may respond and request a hearing within 15 days after service, and the court may enter judgment for any money still due and other just relief. The court must retain the case file for 5 years after the stipulation of dismissal.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-120","t":"Domestic relations actions; scope; mandatory use of court-approved forms by self-represented litigants","s":"Rules 1-120 to 1-128.13 supplement the civil rules for domestic relations actions, and self-represented litigants must use Forms 4A-100 through 4A-315 in dissolution, custody and child support cases (4A-100 to 4A-105 to file and respond, 4A-200 to 4A-215 for temporary relief, 4A-300 to 4A-306 for final orders, and 4A-310 to 4A-315 for default) and Forms 4A-501 through 4A-513 in kinship guardianship cases; courts may distribute only Supreme Court-approved dissolution forms. Self-represented litigants must have Forms 4A-301, 4A-302, 4A-303, 4A-314, 4A-315, 4A-505, 4A-507 and 4-968 notarized before filing or submitting them. Courts must accept approved forms in dissolution cases, and the clerk must accept any filing in a dissolution case without deciding whether it complies with the domestic relations rules and forms.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-121","t":"Temporary domestic orders","s":"In original domestic relations actions where a summons issues, the clerk files a temporary domestic order in substantially the Supreme Court-approved form with the summons, and the petitioner must serve an endorsed copy on the respondent; no order is entered in state child support actions or if the court orders otherwise, and added restrictions beyond the form require notice and hearing. The order binds the petitioner on filing (the petition’s verification must acknowledge understanding it) and the respondent on personal service, and violations may lead to redress including costs and attorney fees. Unless the court orders otherwise, the rule does not apply to Section 40-4-20 property actions, post-decree filings, UIFSA or Uniform Parentage Act proceedings, or third-party custody actions, and it does not bar a Rule 1-066 temporary restraining order.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-122","t":"Dissolution of marriage and Section 40-4-3 NMSA 1978 proceedings; interim order allocating income and expenses","s":"Absent exceptional circumstances, community income and expenses are divided equally while a dissolution or Section 40-4-3 proceeding is pending, and separate income and expenses may also be divided on motion. Unless the parties stipulate to waive it, a party may move for an interim allocation order, or the court may set a hearing on its own, and the parties must exchange the information in Form 4A-212 at least 5 days before the hearing. Any party may move to modify the interim order, and the related statements, orders and notices must be substantially in the Supreme Court-approved forms.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-123","t":"Mandatory disclosure in domestic relations and paternity actions; preliminary disclosure requirements","s":"Unless stipulated and ordered otherwise, in cases involving property or debt division the parties must serve within 45 days after service of the petition a disclosure under Form 4A-212 containing an interim income and expense statement and community and separate property and liability schedules (Forms 4A-212, 4A-214 and 4A-215) with a list of supporting documents. In spousal or child support cases, each side must serve within 45 days of service of process an affidavit of disclosure with the prior year’s tax returns, W-2s, 1099s, work-related daycare statements and dependent medical premiums, 4 months of wage statements, and, for spousal support modification, a Form 4A-212 statement. Sworn supplemental disclosure schedules and child support worksheets must be served on all parties, with copies to the trial judge, at least 5 days before trial, the State need produce only documents it intends to introduce at least 5 days before the hearing, and noncompliance may bring costs, fees or other sanctions.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-124","t":"Child custody; parenting plans; binding arbitration","s":"In domestic relations cases involving custody or visitation, the parties must try to agree on and file a joint parenting plan under Section 40-4-9.1 within 60 days of the filing of the dissolution petition. If no plan is filed, they may agree to binding arbitration under Section 40-4-7.2, and otherwise the court may refer them to family counseling or mediation before a custody or visitation hearing.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-125","t":"Domestic Relations Mediation Act programs","s":"In districts with Domestic Relations Mediation Act programs, if no parenting plan is filed and no binding arbitration is pending, the court may order parties to attend an information session, meet a counselor, mediate, or participate in priority or advisory consultation; successful mediation yields a parenting plan submitted for approval. Objections to priority or advisory consultation recommendations must be filed by motion within 11 days of the filing of the recommendations, responses are due within 11 days after service of the objections, no reply is allowed, and if no objections are filed within 11 days after service the recommendations are adopted by order. Mediation communications and information from court-operated consultations are confidential (with limited exceptions such as legally required reports, the written recommendations, testimony in the case, or a court order for good cause), parties must participate in good faith but are not sanctioned for failing to settle, and the court may order safe exchange or supervised visitation, with fees set by Supreme Court-approved sliding scales based on gross income and ability to pay.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-126","t":"Partial decrees","s":"A partial decree dissolving a marriage is granted only on a showing of exceptional circumstances. The motion must address the decree’s effect on the parties and children, including medical coverage, custody, income division, child and spousal support and taxes, and the decree may include provisions needed to protect the parties and minor children.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-127","t":"Attorney fees in domestic relations cases","s":"A Rule 1-054 motion for attorney fees in a domestic relations case must itemize time spent and affirm that the fees are correct and necessary. The court considers relevant factors including disparity in resources, prior settlement offers, total fees and costs each party spent (including community funds used, balances due and any interim advances ordered), and success on the merits.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128","t":"Uniform collaborative law rules; short title; definitions; applicability","s":"Rules 1-128 to 1-128.13 are the Uniform Collaborative Law Rules and apply to collaborative law participation agreements meeting Rule 1-128.1. The rule defines key terms, including a “collaborative matter” as a dispute or issue arising under Chapter 40 NMSA 1978 (such as divorce, property division, custody, support, adoption, parentage, and premarital or marital agreements) and a “collaborative law communication” as one made to conduct the process after the agreement is signed and before the process concludes.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.1","t":"Collaborative law participation agreement; requirements","s":"A collaborative law participation agreement must be in a signed record and state the parties’ intent to use the collaborative law process, describe the matter, name each collaborative lawyer, and include each lawyer’s confirmation of the representation. The parties may add other provisions consistent with the rules.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.2","t":"Initiation of collaborative law process; voluntary participation; conclusion; termination; notice of discharge or withdrawal of collaborative lawyer; continuation with successor collaborative lawyer","s":"The collaborative process begins when the agreement is signed, a tribunal may not order a party into it over objection, and it concludes on full or agreed partial resolution in a signed record, by a method in the agreement, or by termination. Any party may terminate with or without cause, and the process terminates when a party gives notice in a record, starts a related proceeding or seeks tribunal action without everyone’s agreement, or discharges or loses a collaborative lawyer, who must promptly notify all parties in a record. The process continues if, within 30 days after the discharge or withdrawal notice is sent, the unrepresented party engages a successor collaborative lawyer and a signed record reaffirms and amends the agreement and confirms the new lawyer’s representation.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.3","t":"Proceedings pending before tribunal; status report; dismissal","s":"Parties to a pending proceeding who sign a collaborative agreement must promptly file a certificate or stipulated order of abatement, signed by both counsel and both parties (or it has no effect), stating that they are making significant progress or attempting reconciliation and the case status, which tolls all deadlines. When the process concludes, the parties must promptly file and serve a notice (without stating reasons), which ends the abatement, and any status report the tribunal requires may state only whether the process is ongoing or concluded, with improper communications disregarded. A tribunal must give notice and an opportunity to be heard before dismissing an abated proceeding for delay or failure to prosecute.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.4","t":"Emergency order","s":"Despite a pending collaborative law process, a tribunal may issue any order under the Family Violence Protection Act, Sections 40-13-1 to -12 NMSA 1978.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.5","t":"Adoption of agreement by tribunal","s":"A tribunal may adopt any agreement resulting from a collaborative law process as its order.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.6","t":"Disqualification of collaborative lawyer and lawyers in associated law firm","s":"A collaborative lawyer, and any lawyer in the collaborative lawyer’s law firm, is disqualified from representing a party before a tribunal in a proceeding related to the collaborative matter. The only exception is representing a party to seek an order adopting an agreement reached in the collaborative process.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.7","t":"Disclosure of information","s":"Unless other law provides otherwise, during the collaborative process a party must, on request, make timely, full, candid and informal disclosure of information related to the matter without formal discovery and promptly update information that has materially changed; the parties may define the scope of disclosure.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.8","t":"Standards of professional responsibility and mandatory reporting not affected","s":"The collaborative law rules do not affect lawyers’ or other licensed professionals’ professional obligations, or anyone’s duty under New Mexico law to report abuse, neglect, abandonment or exploitation of a child or adult.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.9","t":"Appropriateness of collaborative law process","s":"Before a prospective party signs a collaborative agreement, the prospective collaborative lawyer must assess whether the process is appropriate (including any history of a coercive or violent relationship) and explain its material benefits and risks compared with alternatives such as litigation, mediation or arbitration. The lawyer must also advise that seeking tribunal intervention terminates the process, participation is voluntary and terminable with or without cause, and the lawyer and the lawyer’s firm cannot represent a party in a related proceeding except to seek adoption of an agreement under Rule 1-128.6(C).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.10","t":"Coercive or violent relationship","s":"Before a prospective party signs a collaborative agreement, the prospective collaborative lawyer must reasonably inquire whether the party has a history of a coercive or violent relationship with another prospective party, and throughout the process the lawyer must continuously and reasonably assess whether such a history exists.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.11","t":"Confidentiality of collaborative law communication","s":"A collaborative law communication is confidential to the extent the parties agree in a signed record or as provided by other New Mexico law.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.12","t":"Privilege against disclosure for collaborative law communication; admissibility; discovery","s":"A party (or a guardian, conservator or personal representative of a party, or a nonparty participant as to that participant’s own communications) may refuse to disclose, and prevent others from disclosing, a collaborative law communication, though otherwise admissible or discoverable evidence does not become privileged merely by use in the process. The privilege is waived only expressly by all parties, in a record or orally in a proceeding, except that a party who discloses a communication waives it to the extent needed for others to respond. There is no privilege for communications required by law to be disclosed, threatening or leading to violence, revealing intent to commit a felony or inflict bodily harm, bearing on whether a binding agreement was reached, or contained in an agreement signed by all parties, and such excepted evidence is not thereby admissible or discoverable for other purposes (the text cross-refers to “Paragraph B or C” for these exceptions, which appear in Paragraph D).","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-128.13","t":"Authority of tribunal in case of noncompliance","s":"Even if Rules 1-128.1, 1-128.9 or 1-128.10 were not complied with, a tribunal may enforce an agreement, apply the Rule 1-128.6 disqualification, or apply the Rule 1-128.12 privilege if it finds the parties signed a record showing intent to enter a collaborative agreement, reasonably believed they were in a collaborative process, and the interests of justice require treating them as such.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-129","t":"Proceedings under the Family Violence Protection Act","s":"The first Family Violence Protection Act petition between two parties proceeds under the civil rules as the original proceeding. Any later petition between the same parties is filed in that original proceeding under the same case number, treated as a paper in the case if it is still pending, or, if closed, the clerk reactivates the original case number and returns the matter to the original judge or that judge’s successor.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-130","t":"Appointment of a treatment guardian","s":"When a professional or physician has proposed mental health treatment and someone believes the respondent lacks capacity to make treatment decisions, an authorized interested person may petition under Section 43-1-15 for a treatment guardian; the petition must include detailed information (diagnosis, symptoms, location, treatment provider and proposed treatment, any emergency medications, the proposed guardian and that person’s receipt of Form 4-931, witnesses, and more) and request appointment for a period not exceeding one year. The hearing must be set no later than 3 business days after the petition is filed (extendable for good cause), and the petition and notice must be served as soon as practicable on the respondent and the respondent’s attorney under Rule 1-004(D)-(F). Unless the respondent knowingly waives a hearing, the respondent must have counsel and may present and cross-examine witnesses, the petitioner must offer sworn professional testimony and proof that the proposed guardian accepts the duties, and on clear and convincing evidence of incapacity the court enters a written order with findings appointing a guardian for up to one year; a substitute guardian may be appointed on motion with a signed Form 4-931 attached.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-131","t":"Notice of federal restriction on right to possess or receive a firearm or ammunition","s":"Certain orders must include written notice that the person is barred by 18 U.S.C. § 922(g)(4) from receiving or possessing firearms or ammunition, that the Administrative Office of the Courts must report the person to the FBI’s National Instant Criminal Background Check System under Section 34-9-19(B), and that the person may petition under Section 34-9-19 to restore the right and remove the listing. The orders are those appointing a full or plenary guardian or conservator with a finding of total incapacity, commitment orders under Sections 43-1-11 to -13, involuntary protective services or placement orders under Section 27-7-26, and assisted outpatient treatment orders with a finding of serious violent behavior or threatened or attempted serious physical harm.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-140","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; mandatory use of forms","s":"On appointing a guardian or conservator, the court must file a separate Form 4-993 order identifying who is entitled to notice and access to records, and on appointing a conservator a separate Form 4-994 order requiring or waiving bond under Section 45-5-411. Unless waived, a conservator must file a Form 4-995 notice of bonding, with a Form 4-995.1 corporate surety statement, each time an inventory or report is filed. Guardians use Form 4-996 for 90-day, annual and final reports, conservators use Form 4-997 for the 90-day inventory and Form 4-998 for annual and final reports, and petitioners use Form 4-999 for notice of hearing and rights.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-141","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; determination of persons entitled to notice of proceedings or access to court records","s":"Any court determination of who is entitled to notice of a guardianship or conservatorship proceeding or access to its records must be made by a separate written order that addresses no other matter.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-142","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; proof of certification of professional guardians and conservators","s":"A professional guardian or conservator is one serving more than two unrelated individuals (not related by marriage, adoption, or third degree of blood or affinity). The order appointing one must require proof that the individual assigned the duties is certified and in good standing with the Center for Guardianship Certification, due by the earlier of 90 days after the appointment order or the filing of the initial report or inventory, and proof of good standing must be submitted annually. Those appointed before the rule’s effective date had 6 months from that date to provide proof.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-143","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; appointment of visitor, qualified health care professional, and guardian ad litem; timing and review of reports","s":"On a petition to appoint a guardian or conservator, the court must appoint a qualified health care professional, a visitor and, if necessary, a guardian ad litem, with all time periods counted in calendar days under Rule 1-006(A)(1). Reports are due no later than 14 days before the hearing (health care professional), 11 days before (visitor) and 7 days before (guardian ad litem), and the petitioner must provide copies within 3 days of filing to the alleged incapacitated person, the visitor, the guardian ad litem, attorneys of record, any agent under a power of attorney (unless ordered otherwise) and others the court designates. Before the hearing, the guardian ad litem must review the reports and their significance with the alleged incapacitated person.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-144","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; mandatory viewing of New Mexico Courts’ Guardian and Conservator Orientation Program videos","s":"Before appointment, every proposed guardian or conservator, including professional ones (those serving more than two unrelated individuals), must view the listed New Mexico Courts’ orientation videos: 6 for guardians and 8 for conservators. The proposed guardian or conservator must file a certificate of viewing no later than 5 calendar days before the hearing on the petition.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-145","t":"Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; professional guardians and conservators; procedures and time limits for filing reports and financial statements","s":"A professional guardian or conservator must file reports substantially in compliance with Forms 4-996, 4-997 and 4-998, the annual report within 30 days after the appointment anniversary and other reports within 60 days after resignation, removal or termination (or a longer reasonable time set by the court), serving the protected person, any guardian or conservator, and persons listed on Form 4-993. Each professional conservator report, and any professional guardian report containing financial documents, requires a contemporaneous separate confidential filing of unredacted third-party financial statements with a Form 4-998.1 cover sheet stating the page count; it is automatically sealed and, absent court order, disclosed only to the State Auditor. The court forwards conservator reports to the State Auditor within 5 days, the Auditor responds within 15 business days (declining, accepting an audit, or requesting more information), the conservator must answer a request within 15 business days (extendable by up to 15 more for good cause), the Auditor then decides within 15 business days, and any audit report is due within 90 days; a conservator’s lapse leads to a status conference at the conservator’s own cost and possible contempt, and Auditor subpoena costs are borne by the conservator, not the estate.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-150","t":"Scope; forms","s":"Rules 1-150 to 1-156 govern all kinship guardianship petitions, while a petition concerning a child with at least one living parent is governed by the Domestic Relations Code, and cases involving the Children, Youth and Families Department may be assigned to the family or children’s court division as the chief judge or local rule provides. Self-represented litigants must use Forms 4A-501 through 4A-513, and attorneys’ pleadings must be consistent with the rules and forms.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-151","t":"Petition; parties; intervention; joinder and sealing","s":"Only a “caregiver” as defined in Section 40-10B-3 may file a verified kinship guardianship petition, which must allege facts under Section 40-10B-5(B) and attach any guardianship assistance agreement with the Children, Youth and Families Department under seal. When the department is a party it must be served under Rule 1-004; it may intervene if it has court-ordered custody, must answer like other respondents if it has custody other than by voluntary placement or guardianship assistance agreement, and must be joined if it has custody by voluntary placement agreement plus a guardianship assistance agreement, in which case it files sealed filings (its response, including Indian Child Welfare Act compliance, the placement agreement, and notices of revocation or of any abuse or neglect petition). The court may seal department-related information, and when the department is joined and petitioners are self-represented under an unrevoked assistance agreement, the department must handle and pay for service on the parents, request settings, give notice (including to the tribe), issue subpoenas, prepare consent forms with a notary and its own Form 4A-514 consent, and draft orders.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-152","t":"Mandatory and discretionary appointment of guardian ad litem; powers and duties of the guardian ad litem; payment","s":"The court must appoint a guardian ad litem when a participating parent objects to a kinship guardianship petition or when a parent seeks revocation and the guardian objects, and may appoint one on motion or on its own. Appointed by order substantially in Form 4A-515, the guardian ad litem investigates (including home visits and interviews), reports on the child’s best interests and position, and recommends a transition plan if the petition is denied or the guardianship revoked. The court may order parties to pay a reasonable fee or, if all parties are indigent, pay it from court funds.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-153","t":"Advisement of rights","s":"At the respondent’s first appearance in a kinship guardianship case, the court must inform the respondent of the right to interpretation, the parties (including whether the Children, Youth and Families Department is one), the petition’s allegations, the right to a trial, and the consequences if the allegations are proven, including possible child support.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-154","t":"Kinship guardianship hearing; elements of proof; judgment","s":"The Rules of Evidence apply, and the petitioner must prove by clear and convincing evidence jurisdiction and venue, service on and notice to the parents, that appointment is in the child’s best interest, for a child aged 14 or older that any nominee of the child is in the child’s best interest and the child has not objected in writing, that the petitioner is qualified under Section 40-10B-5(A), and that no Uniform Probate Code guardian exists. For each parent, the petitioner must also prove written unwithdrawn consent, termination or suspension of parental rights, the child’s residence with the petitioner without the parent for 90 days or more immediately before filing plus the custodial parent’s unwillingness or inability to provide adequate care, or extraordinary circumstances. If the elements are not proven, the court may dismiss or make another disposition in the child’s best interest, and in a judgment it may order a parent to pay support the parent can afford and order visitation in the child’s best interest.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-155","t":"Appointment of guardian","s":"On appointing a kinship guardian, the court issues letters of guardianship stating, without unnecessary information, that the guardian has all parental authority except consent to adoption and any rights the court expressly reserves to a parent. If the Children, Youth and Families Department is a party, orders likely to be shown to non-parties use only the caption “In the Matter of [initials of child]” with the case number; otherwise the caption uses the child’s initials and names the petitioner and respondents.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"},{"n":"1-156","t":"Successor guardian","s":"If a kinship guardian dies or becomes incapacitated, the new caregiver may file a new petition. The court is not bound by successor-guardian terms in a guardianship assistance agreement with the Children, Youth and Families Department, but under Section 40-10B-19(B) the department may have to pay a qualified successor guardian’s costs of obtaining a subsidized guardianship, up to the limits in the Kinship Guardianship Act.","u":"https://nmonesource.com/nmos/nmra/en/item/5687/index.do"}]});
